('What are we told in the first paragraph?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. The London Corresponding Society Alternatives The London Corresponding Society (LCS) was established in 1792 by Tho- 31. mas Hardy for promoting the reform of Parliament. Its aims were relatively A resisting modest, 31_____ universal suffrage, annual parliaments, wages for MPs B destroying and the freedom to form popular associations. Its membership was drawn C including mainly from artisans and tradesmen, and its methods were decidedly non- D glorifying violent. It wanted to debate, lobby, correspond with like-minded organi- sations and achieve change through reform 32_____ bloody revolution. 32. Nevertheless, from its birth, the LCS attracted Government interest. The A in connection with extent of this, and the means by which the Government gathered informa- tion on the LCS and its membership, are 33_____ by Government files now lodged in the Public Record Office. They include detailed accounts of Hardy’s trial for high treason following his arrest in 1794. Hardy had 33. the benefit of a trial by jury – a right denied to many after 1794 – and was acquitted. Trial by jury was a much-prized right in Britain. As John Ashley, the Secretary of the LCS, wrote in a pamphlet in December 1795, ‘Let us D revealed remember that we are not YET deprived of trial by jury: The English juries have long been celebrated for their 34_____ cruel laws.’ There was indeed 34. a great reluctance among the public to punish people for merely expressing A opposition to their opinions. On the other hand, letters, often anonymous, denouncing B dependence on people for uttering 35_____ comments, are also held in the Public Record C approval of Office. These evoke the tense tone of the time when fear of betrayal created D liberation from a paranoid atmosphere. 35. Dan Cruickshank, BBC History A pro-Government The Plague of Nations From a review of a book by Helen Epstein Halfway through The Invisible Cure, Helen Epstein tend to be too self-important, too polemical, too grim writes about finding a long-forgotten document in a and too at odds with my experiences in the field. Epstein, small research library in Canada. Reading through the in contrast, teaches me things I didn’t know. Her rigo- paper, Epstein says, “I felt as though a small stick of rous reporting unearths new findings among old, worn- dynamite had gone off my head.” Epstein had unearthed out issues. And the evidence she puts forward could pro- a rare copy of a detailed study on the sexual behaviour vide a roadmap for comprehensive prevention programs of Ugandans in the late 1980s and early 90s, a period that incorporate teaching abstinence, using condoms and, that coincided with the country’s historic drop in HIV most critically, emphasizing fidelity. rates. In short, Epstein knew, the research done by Max- Epstein began her work in Africa in 1993 as a scientist ine Ankrah, an African-American academic, would give for a biotechnology company working in Uganda on an invaluable insights into what had halted the epidemic AIDS vaccine. Epstein often focuses on failures, including – insights that then could be applied to other countries her own early search for a vaccine. “In science, failures are with high rates of HIV and AIDS. often as important as successes, because they tell us where Before Epstein’s discovery, Ankrah’s research seemed the limits are”, she', '274c49a4d13f', '["Maxine Ankrah had published a well-known book on HIV and sexual behaviour","American and Canadian research on AIDS tended to focus on different issues","Helen Epstein had discovered relevant and thorough research on AIDS and HIV","Many AIDS researchers had overemphasised the importance of sexual behaviour"]'::jsonb, 'C', '2012vt_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2012vt', 4, 36),
