ut walking speed was not. It seems that even moderate levels of physical activity can serve to limit declines in cognition in older adults. The Power of Music I have often wondered what it is about music that eli and non-autistic children, comparing those with similar cits emotions. Philosophers and biologists have asked the language skills, and asked the kids to match the music to drawn to music. ferent feelings in music did not depend on their diagno- Some scientists conclude that music’s influence may sis. Autistic and typical children with similar verbal skills be a chance event, arising from its ability to hijack brain performed equally well, indicating that music can reliably systems built for other purposes such as language, emo- convey feelings even in people whose ability to pick up tions and movement. As Harvard University psycholo- emotion-laden social cues, such as facial expressions or gist Steven Pinker famously puts it, music is “auditory tone of voice, is severely compromised. cheesecake”, a confection crafted to tickle the areas of the Music’s ability to convey feelings may underlie one of its mind that evolved for more important functions. But as most important benefits. In most cultures, music is always a result of chance, music seems to offer a novel system a communal event: everyone gets together to sing, dance of communication rooted in emotions rather than mea- and play instruments. Even in Western societies, which ning. Recent d', 'a4ecc0ce7c5a', '["Older women taking part in physical activities improve their short-term memory","Ordinary exercise is beneficial for the intellec tual functions of older people","Women over 65 have been medically assessed in order to evaluate their mental status","Mental health does not seem to be related to people’s physical activities d non-autistic children, comparing those with similar nguage skills, and asked the kids to match the music to otions. They found that kids’ ability to recognize dif- rent feelings in music did not depend on their diagno- s. Autistic and typical children with similar verbal skills rformed equally well, indicating that music can reliably nvey feelings even in people whose ability to pick up otion-laden social cues, such as facial expressions or ne of voice, is severely compromised. Music’s ability to convey feelings may underlie one of its st important benefits. In most cultures, music is always communal event: everyone gets together to sing, dance d play instruments. Even in Western societies, which iquely differentiate musical performers from listeners, ople enjoy music together in a wide variety of settings: ncing at a wedding or a night club, singing hymns in urch, crooning with their kids, Christmas carolling and nging “Happy Birthday” at a party. The popularity of ch rituals suggests that music confers social cohesive- ss, perhaps by creating empathetic connections among mbers of a group. Music also bestows advantages on us as individuals. derlying our conscious impressions of a tune are psy- ological effects that can improve our mental and phy- cal well-being. Studies show that upbeat, tense or excit- g music can physically excite the listener, triggering the dy’s fight and flight response: heart and breathing rates crease, a person may break out in a sweat, and adrena- ne enters the blood-stream. This “pumping up” effect plains why so many people enjoy listening to rock while ey work out – the music primes the psychological sys- ms needed for high-energy movement. Energizing melo- es tend to boost our mood in general, waking us up if are feeling tired and creating a sense of excitement in y situation. On the other hand, music can be calming, ducing the levels of the stress hormone cortisol in the ood, lowering heart and respiration rates, and alleviating in, according to several studies. Karen Schrock, Scientific American Mind"]'::jsonb, 'B', '2013ht_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2013ht', 1, 32),
