('What can be concluded from the first paragraph?', 'ELF', 'verbal', '"In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Parkinson’s Disease Alternatives British physician and pharmacist James Parkinson first described the disease that 31. now bears his name in 1817. The cardinal symptom is a general and progressive A pronounced difficulty with movement, followed by deterioration in mental function. B nonspecific When diagnosed early, symptoms of Parkinson’s disease can be managed C characteristic fairly successfully with drugs for as long as eight to 15 years. But often the D irrelevant malady is not recognized soon enough, because it begins with very 31_____ symptoms. Tense muscles in an arm or shoulder, for example, tend to send 32. people to orthopedists, not neurologists. Fatigue, depression or sudden out- A at least breaks of sweating are typically pegged to other common problems. These conditions often remain mild for a long time', '5684871b4107', '[" nine to 12 years can pass before the disease 32_____ asserts itself. But by then a large percentage of cells in certain brain regions have already died. Individuals begin to lose 33. their fine-motor coordination: they cannot thread a needle, and their hand- writing becomes tiny and hard to read. Soon everyday tasks such as combing hair, buttoning a shirt or tying shoes become impossible. Patients become 33_____ others, and their quality of life declines dramatically. As move- D intolerant of ment slows, so do mental processes. Thinking drags, and speech drawls. Scientists have known since the late 1920s that 34_____ the disease 34. advances, neurons in the midbrain die off. Neuroscientists still cannot say A unless definitively what triggers the trouble. In some cases, physical brain damage B as from accidents or blows to the head from boxing, for example, may start C although the process. Heavy metals and pesticides have also been implicated by some D why epidemiological and animal studies. Though lacking a culprit, experts concur that some mechanism causes brain 35. proteins to misfold, which in turn kills neurons. A genetic defect is 35_____ in A involved five to ten percent of cases, and the aberrations provide interesting clues. B affected Konrad Schmidt & Wolfgang Oertel, Scientific American D caused Intelligences Popular science writers face a dilemma. Laypeople come to scientific concept of “cognition” involves complex inte- science with their own systematic ideas often far removed ractions between many varied computational and neu- from scientific ones. To explain science effectively, we need rological mechanisms with long developmental histo- to start from these everyday intuitions and move readers ries. Ultimately, these complex interactions allow people towards the scientific concepts. Yet we often end up simply to make their way in the world. The popular concept of reinforcing the initial intuitions rather than overturning “intelligence” is much like the popular biological concepts them. No matter how carefully we report and interpret, of “energy” or “vitality”: it sees a complicated set of fun- the reader’s final picture will often be quite different from ctional relations as a single force. the one we set out to paint. Most developmental or cognitive psychologists simply Popular science may even create fabulous intellec- do not talk about intelligence at all, singular or plural. tual monsters: chimeras of scientific and everyday ideas They talk, rather, about “intuitive sy\"","Popular science writers rely primarily on intuition","Science is most efficient if based on laypeople’s ideas","It is rather difficult to explain science to laypeople","Readers’ intuitive notions are often confirmed in scientific reports"]'::jsonb, 'C', '2013ht_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2013ht', 4, 