from boxing, for example, may start C although the process. Heavy metals and pesticides have also been implicated by some D why epidemiological and animal studies. Though lacking a culprit, experts concur that some mechanism causes brain 35. proteins to misfold, which in turn kills neurons. A genetic defect is 35_____ in A involved five to ten percent of cases, and the aberrations provide interesting clues. B affected Konrad Schmidt & Wolfgang Oertel, Scientific American D caused Intelligences Popular science writers face a dilemma. Laypeople come to scientific concept of “cognition” involves complex inte- science with their own systematic ideas often far removed ractions between many varied computational and neu- from scientific ones. To explain science effectively, we need rological mechanisms with long developmental histo- to start from these everyday intuitions and move readers ries. Ultimately, these complex interactions allow people towards the scientific concepts. Yet we often end up simply to make their way in the world. The popular concept of reinforcing the initial intuitions rather than overturning “intelligence” is much like the popular biological concepts them. No matter how carefully we report and interpret, of “energy” or “vitality”: it sees a complicated set of fun- the reader’s final picture will often be quite different from ctional relations as a single force. the one we set out to paint. Most developmental or cognitive psychologists simply Popular science may even create fabulous intellec- do not talk about intelligence at all, singular or plural. tual monsters: chimeras of scientific and everyday ideas They talk, rather, about “intuitive sy\"","Popular science is falsified information about research in social sciences","Popular science is interpreted according to people’s own opinions","Popular science is focused on people’s social and intellectual background","Popular science is common beliefs presented by psychologists"]'::jsonb, 'B', '2013ht_