('What is suggested about online advertising in China?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Today’s Fiction Question Our planet is getting mad as hell and it isn’t going to take 31. What is the main point here? it any longer. It’s an old theme but a rich one, and in the 1950s and 60s it provided plots for dozens of science-fic- A Environmental fiction is now a regular part of tion disaster novels. Cities were drowned, oceans eradica- contemporary literature. ted and pastures killed off as authors subjected civilisation B Science fiction and eco-thrillers are pure to indignities – apocalyptic literature that mirrored the storytelling. era’s cold war uncertainties. Today, this kind of storytelling C Environmental fiction is best described as books has taken on a harder edge and eco-thrillers have become on catastrophes and wars. a more robust genre on the page and the screen. Environ- mental fiction is moving away from its roots in science fiction in its criticism of modern society. fiction and becoming part of mainstream literature – as is revealed by some of the most recent novels to tackle climate change and the like. Online Advertising Question Digital media’s big sale push worldwide may centre on 32. What is suggested about online how easy it is for marketers to measure the performance advertising in China? of online advertising campaigns, but such issues tend to get pushed to the side in China, home to the world’s lar- A It is evaluated in the same way as in the rest of gest internet population. Most online advertising in the the world. Middle Kingdom, much like neighbouring Japan and B Medium-sized businesses cannot afford online Korea, is not priced by how many people have seen an ad, advertising. or how many people were intrigued enough to click on C It is nowadays less frequently used than in Japan. it, but how long an ad is posted on a site. There has been no shortage of forecasting over the years, predicting the online advertising. demise of a pricing model criticized as an antiquated old media legacy by detractors, but the reason it is still used in China is because that’s the way most online advertisers, usually small and medium-sized companies with limited resources, like it. Cost-per-day not only has the benefit of familiarity, but with credible data thin on the ground it also provides a relatively reliable way of gauging the reach of digital campaigns. Lost Women of Science In December 1788, the astronomer royal, Dr Nevil Mas- women’s exclusion from learned bodies. She questioned kelyne, Fellow of the Royal Society, wrote to 38-year-old the Baconian notion of relentless mechanical progress Caroline Herschel congratulating her on being “the first in her polemic Observations on Experimental Philosophy woman in the history of the world” to discover not one, (1668). She also produced arguably the first-ever science- but two new comets. Her celebrity would, as the director fiction story, The Blazing World (1666), which considered of the Paris Observatory noted, “shine down through the the alternative futures of science. All this earned her the ages”. sobriquet “Mad Madge”. Dr Maskelyne hoped Caroline Herschel did not feel The first original paper that might be considered as part isolated among the male community of astronomers in of a scientific research programme conducted by a woman Britain and “for the benefit of terrestrial astronomy, will and published in the Royal Society’s journ', '9f5a60f1e469', '["It is evaluated in the same way as in the rest of the world.","Medium-sized businesses cannot afford online advertising.","It is nowadays less frequently used than in Japan.","Medium-sized businesses prefer time-based online advertising. women’s exclusion from learned bodies. She questioned the Baconian notion of relentless mechanical progress in her polemic Observations on Experimental Philosophy (1668). She also produced arguably the first-ever science- fiction story, The Blazing World (1666), which considered the alternative futures of science. All this earned her the sobriquet “Mad Madge”. The first original paper that might be considered as part of a scientific research programme conducted by a woman and published in the Royal Society’s journal, Philosophi- cal Transactions, concerned extra-terrestrial phenomena. It was by Caroline Herschel (sister to William Herschel, the great Romantic astronomer) in August 1786. Caroline Herschel’s speciality was discovering comets, of which she found eight at a time when fewer than 30 were known. Her brother was immensely proud of her, built her special telescopes and helped her to obtain the first state salary for a female astronomer in Britain. Caroline Herschel also kept an observational journal for more than 30 years. It is one of the earliest records of how science actually gets done, its secret tribulations as well as its public triumphs. By the turn of the century, major science was indeed done by women. Heartha Ayrton was producing highly original work on the electric arc. Although rejected as a fellow in 1902, she was the first woman to read her own paper at a Royal Society meeting two years later and was awarded the Royal Society’s Hughes medal in 1906. Slowly the tide was turning. These remarkable women had already added a third dimension to the whole scientific enterprise as originally conceived by Lord Bacon, and by the founding fathers of the Royal Society. The two primary aims of science had long been established as the discovery of the nature of physical reality “by experiment and proof ”, and the app- lications of such discoveries “for the relief of man’s estate”. The third, fundamental imperative is that science should disperse the seeds of knowledge to all and as imaginatively as possible. Until science knowledge was explained, explo- red and widely understood, the work of scientists would always be incomplete. Richard Holmes, The Guardian Weekly"]'::jsonb, 'D', '2014ht_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2014ht', 1, 32);
