('What is implied about the Pacific in the opening paragraph?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Warranties Alternatives Customers tend to agonise over the relative merits of different models of 31. electronic goods such as digital cameras or plasma televisions. But when A during they get to the till, many spend freely on something they barely think about B against at all: an extended warranty, which is often more profitable to the retailer C beyond than the device it covers. D despite Shoppers typically pay 10–50% of the cost of a product to insure it 31_____ the term covered by the manufacturer’s guarantee. The terms of 32. these deals vary (and there is often a great deal of fine print), but they usually A consistently promise to repair or replace a faulty product for between one and four years. Yet products 32_____ break within the period covered, and repairs tend to cost no more than the warranty itself. That makes warranties amazingly profitable: they generate some $15 billion annually for American retailers, 33. according to Warranty Week, a trade journal. So why, asks a paper published in the Journal of Consumer Research, do so many customers still buy extended warranties? The authors examined purchase 33_____ from a big electronics retailer for over 600 households. D prices They concluded that the decision to buy a warranty had a great deal to do with a shopper’s mood. 34. If customers are about to buy something fun (e.g., a plasma television A splash out rather than a vacuum cleaner), they will be more inclined to 34_____ on B cash in extra insurance. This is because customers value “hedonistic” items over C hold back utilitarian ones, regardless of the actual price tag. D stand by The popularity of warranties should logically depend on the likelihood of a product’s failure, says one of the researchers. But 35_____ most poli- 35. cies go unused, he admits that the emotional tranquillity that comes with A if buying a new warranty is not in itself without value, even if “rationally, it B after doesn’t make sense”. C unless The Economist Pacific Islanders Nicholas Thomas’s new book, Islanders: The Pacific in the Age islanders were outward looking and curious. Not only was of Empire, conceptualises the Pacific as an ocean highway the Pacific a place rather than a space, it was a cosmopoli- crisscrossed by the routes of incessant voyaging. Foremost tan world in which islanders, Europeans, east Asians, black among these voyagers are the Pacific islanders themselves, Americans and others met and mixed, entered and left. not just those who migrated eastwards several thousand These voyages were social and conceptual as well as years ago in voyages unique for their range and rapidity, geographic. Travellers to and from the Pacific experienced but those who from the late 18th century travelled across new worlds that changed how they thought and felt, and and beyond the ocean, and those who had always moved carried home stories which influenced the communities, between its various archipelagos. European voyagers, trad- island or continent from which they derived. Thomas ers, missionaries, and colonisers are given their due but have presents the history of the Pacific as a compound of the no special precedence in Thomas’s account. many stories told by voyagers, inc', '2510bf03dc9a', '["Its history of travelling is shorter than was once thought.","It was first explored by seafaring people from various European countries.","Its far-off islands were practically unknown before 1800.","It has been crossed by different kinds of people since ancient times."]'::jsonb, 'D', '2014ht_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2014ht', 4, 36),
