('In what way can the fighting in Lucknow be characterized as somewhat paradoxical, according to the text?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'New Scotland Yard Question New Scotland Yard may be a beacon of law and order in 31. What is implied here? the heart of London, but the sight of its lights burning through the night has taken on a different meaning. Staff A The Metropolitan Police are working hard to cut at the HQ of the Metropolitan Police use so much ligh- down on greenhouse gases. ting, heating, cooling and electricity that the tower pumps B New Scotland Yard need to reassess their out 13,491 tonnes of carbon dioxide a year – equivalent to consumption of electricity. about 2,200 households. It makes it the most polluting C The Metropolitan Police have never been a police station in England and Wales and one of the biggest guiding light for people in London. contributors to greenhouse gases of any public building D New Scotland Yard have lost the confidence of in Britain. the British people. Animal Life Question It took a mere 85 million years – the geologic blink of an 32. What is said about evolution in this text? eye – for animals to evolve and radiate out over much of the world’s land and oceans. Although fossil records and A Historically, many animals have early global molecular biology have provided much information on warming to thank for their existence. the spread of animal life, scientists have not been able to B The extensive spread of animals across our globe figure out exactly what sparked this massive diversifica- millions of years ago led to global warming. tion. New research shows that nutrient-rich runoff from C The original spread of animal life throughout the massive melting glaciers may have provided the extra planet was probably helped by melting ice. energy needed to fuel this dramatic evolution. D Millions of years ago, melting ice almost put an end to animal life on earth. Africans in the Indian Mutiny Throughout 2007, the 200th anniversary of the abolition ordered and bought 18 slaves, who arrived at Bombay in of slavery, the focus was naturally on the shipment of Afri- an Arab ship and who were brought overland in covered can slaves to the West Indies, but no mention was made of carriages. Wajid Ali Shah’s own father ordered 200 swords the trade eastwards from Africa to the Indian subcontinent for his African troops. So the custom of employing Afri- or of the Arab traders who sold slaves to wealthy Indian cans and an overland slave-trading route was already estab- rulers. Although a few Africans rose to positions of power lished. As many as 1,000 male and female African slaves in India, some even establishing small kingdoms, most may have arrived in Lucknow in 1847 and 1848. Although entered the country as slaves. Their descendants are found Africans were employed elsewhere in India, the Nawabs of today in Gujarat and along the southern coast. But little Oudh seem unique in importing women as well as men. is known about those slaves shipped in through Bombay In 1860, with the British firmly back in control, the and Karachi and taken eastwards to be sold. remaining Africans in Lucknow petitioned the Chief Anecdotal stories of African soldiers fighting against Commissioner for pensions. This was not a completely the British during the Indian Mutiny (also known as the futile hope because many people who had been dependent Indian Uprising) occur in military memoirs and par-', '762c63aa2bf7', '["The Indians and the Africans took part in the conflict for different reasons.","The British soldiers did not expect African women to serve as soldiers in Wajid Ali Shah’s army.","The Africans defended their slave owner against the anti-slavery British.","The British were only able to defeat Wajid Ali Shah with the help of his former slaves. AND HERE ARE SOME SHORTER TEXTS: TV Psychology There has clearly been an explosion in TV psychology in the past few years. The schedules are crammed with pro- grammes showcasing different kinds of therapy. Makeover shows for the soul are the latest version of reality TV and often make for compelling viewing. However, therapists’ views are divided on this new phenomenon. Some worry about the dumbing down and exploitation that sometimes takes place, whereas others are grateful that the potential of therapy, and a more psychologically aware approach to life, is being brought to a wider audience. But ethical ques- tions do arise. Often these programmes unearth deeper emotional issues of which the participants were unaware when they originally agreed to take part. Teenagers and Language Since 1915, adults in the U.S. have made huge vocabulary gains and young people only modest ones. Now, that’s a symptom of the growing potency of teenage subculture. Rather than being socialized into the adult speech com- munity, teenagers are resistant. They can understand what you say but they’re reluctant to use your language and they want to retreat into their own dialect. In 1950, I could both understand my parents’ language and use it. Teenage sub- culture is a modern phenomenon and quite bizarre. I was 16 years old in 1950 and it never occurred to any of us that we were in some blessed state that we wanted to perpetuate. – 14 Meanings Consider the following statements: “War continues.” “No sign of peace.” Does our brain treat these two sentences differently, despite their identical meaning? A new study suggests it does. British researchers showed that we are better at detecting words that carry negative meaning than those that are positive. Volunteers were exposed to a word for a fraction of a second – too short a time to consciously read the word – and then asked to guess whether the word was neutral or had emotional content (either positive or negative). The subjects were most accurate at detecting the negative words. The mechanisms underlying this phe- nomenon are not clear, but researchers speculate that the brain might process negative stimuli faster than positive ones. A different explanation could be that information processing is equally fast for both types of information but that negative words better capture our attention, causing the processing to start earlier. – 15"]'::jsonb, 'C', '2015ht_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2015ht', 1, 35),
