('What is said about evolution in this text?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'New Scotland Yard Question New Scotland Yard may be a beacon of law and order in 31. What is implied here? the heart of London, but the sight of its lights burning through the night has taken on a different meaning. Staff A The Metropolitan Police are working hard to cut at the HQ of the Metropolitan Police use so much ligh- down on greenhouse gases. ting, heating, cooling and electricity that the tower pumps B New Scotland Yard need to reassess their out 13,491 tonnes of carbon dioxide a year – equivalent to consumption of electricity. about 2,200 households. It makes it the most polluting C The Metropolitan Police have never been a police station in England and Wales and one of the biggest guiding light for people in London. contributors to greenhouse gases of any public building D New Scotland Yard have lost the confidence of in Britain. the British people. Animal Life Question It took a mere 85 million years – the geologic blink of an 32. What is said about evolution in this text? eye – for animals to evolve and radiate out over much of the world’s land and oceans. Although fossil records and A Historically, many animals have early global molecular biology have provided much information on warming to thank for their existence. the spread of animal life, scientists have not been able to B The extensive spread of animals across our globe figure out exactly what sparked this massive diversifica- millions of years ago led to global warming. tion. New research shows that nutrient-rich runoff from C The original spread of animal life throughout the massive melting glaciers may have provided the extra planet was probably helped by melting ice. energy needed to fuel this dramatic evolution. D Millions of years ago, melting ice almost put an end to animal life on earth. Africans in the Indian Mutiny Throughout 2007, the 200th anniversary of the abolition ordered and bought 18 slaves, who arrived at Bombay in of slavery, the focus was naturally on the shipment of Afri- an Arab ship and who were brought overland in covered can slaves to the West Indies, but no mention was made of carriages. Wajid Ali Shah’s own father ordered 200 swords the trade eastwards from Africa to the Indian subcontinent for his African troops. So the custom of employing Afri- or of the Arab traders who sold slaves to wealthy Indian cans and an overland slave-trading route was already estab- rulers. Although a few Africans rose to positions of power lished. As many as 1,000 male and female African slaves in India, some even establishing small kingdoms, most may have arrived in Lucknow in 1847 and 1848. Although entered the country as slaves. Their descendants are found Africans were employed elsewhere in India, the Nawabs of today in Gujarat and along the southern coast. But little Oudh seem unique in importing women as well as men. is known about those slaves shipped in through Bombay In 1860, with the British firmly back in control, the and Karachi and taken eastwards to be sold. remaining Africans in Lucknow petitioned the Chief Anecdotal stories of African soldiers fighting against Commissioner for pensions. This was not a completely the British during the Indian Mutiny (also known as the futile hope because many people who had been dependent Indian Uprising) occur in military memoirs and par-', '762c63aa2bf7', '["Historically, many animals have early global warming to thank for their existence.","The extensive spread of animals across our globe millions of years ago led to global warming.","The original spread of animal life throughout the planet was probably helped by melting ice.","\"Millions of years ago, melting ice almost put an end to animal life on earth. ordered and bought 18 slaves, who arrived at Bombay in an Arab ship and who were brought overland in covered carriages. Wajid Ali Shah’s own father ordered 200 swords for his African troops. So the custom of employing Afri- cans and an overland slave-trading route was already estab- lished. As many as 1,000 male and female African slaves may have arrived in Lucknow in 1847 and 1848. Although Africans were employed elsewhere in India, the Nawabs of Oudh seem unique in importing women as well as men. In 1860, with the British firmly back in control, the remaining Africans in Lucknow petitioned the Chief Commissioner for pensions. This was not a completely futile hope because many people who had been dependent on the Nawab for their livelihood did receive small sums from the British government in compensation for losing their source of income. However, the Africans’ petition was turned down and over the next two decades their pleas became more urgent as their numbers dwindled. A large number were said to have died from starvation, while oth- ers had left the city. The care and financial support given by the British government to slaves in Bombay contrasts starkly with the official indifference shown to the Africans in Lucknow. For backing the ‘wrong side’ during the Mutiny they were still being punished 20 years later. Only one sympathetic voice was heard in their defence. Sir William Muir, head of the Intelligence Department during the Mutiny, decla- red in May 1877: “The fact that they fought against us at Lucknow ought not to be brought against them – it was probably natural"," the fact that they fought bravely is surely in their favour!” African men and women came to Oudh as slaves to satisfy the whims of the last king, who then left them unprovided for when he was deposed. They fought bravely in the Mutiny, a fight which was not of their making, and have been paying the price ever since. Rosie Llewellyn-Jones, History Today\""]'::jsonb, 'C', '2015ht_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2015ht', 1, 32),
