re D They are a result of people’s unethical drive to edifice of capitalism is based on capital – which is really make more money than they need. just another word for confidence. If people believe your confidence to be authentic, the risk you take is likely to be small. But as soon as people think you are bluffing, they panic – and panic destroys wealth faster than confidence can ever increase it. Verbaldel ELF16A V1.indd 11 2016-01-07 13:27:46 New Orleans When Hurricane Katrina hit New Orleans in late August asters and an almost constant sense of flux. Partly this was 2005, the city and its people suffered wounds that are still a result of its location. Or, as New Orleans Mayor Marc healing. While the rebuilding efforts continue apace, every Morial put it in 2001: ‘This was a lousy place to put a city.’ one seems to agree that the city that eventually emerges Unsurprisingly, therefore, the city’s particular relationship from the wreckage will be very different to the “old” New with catastrophe has long been proverbial. Orleans. But it was not just the physical city that was As bad as hurricanes and conflagrations and potential devastated by Katrina. Equally damaged was its carefully slave rebellions could be, perhaps no single threat was cultivated image as the city that care forgot. Shocked by more pressing and more characteristic of 19th-century the indelible images of destructio', 'd28608502767', '["Then, as now, hurricanes were regarded as the biggest threat to life and property.","The consequences of racial uprisings were looked upon as more serious than those of hurricanes and flooding.","Epidemics of yellow fever seem to have been feared much more than natural disasters.","\"Repeated outbreaks of cholera caused more deaths than any other disease. Verbaldel ELF16A V1.indd 13 AND HERE ARE SOME SHORTER TEXTS: Music and Mind Nearly 20 years ago a small study advanced the notion that listening to Mozart’s Sonata for Two Pianos in D Major could boost mental functioning. It was not long before trademarked “Mozart effect” products appealed to neu- rotic parents aiming to put toddlers on the fast track to the Ivy League. Georgia’s governor even proposed giving every newborn there a classical CD or cassette. The evidence for Mozart therapy turned out to be flimsy, perhaps nonexist- ent, although the original study never claimed anything more than a temporary and limited effect. In recent years, however, neuroscientists have examined the benefits of a concerted effort to study and practice music, as opposed to playing a Mozart CD or a computer-based “brain fit- ness” game once in a while. Advanced monitoring tech- niques have enabled scientists to see what happens inside your head when you listen to your mother and actually practice the violin for an hour every afternoon. And they have found that music lessons can produce profound and lasting changes that enhance the general ability to learn. These results should disabuse public officials of the idea that music classes are a mere frill, ripe for discarding in the budget crises that constantly beset public schools. The Impostor Phenomenon The impostor phenomenon is defined as the mistaken feeling that one’s successes are unearned and that at any moment the charade could end. The emphasis is on “mis- taken” – these people are not really frauds. Or are they? Researchers asked this question recently. They studied how often self-defined impostors engaged in plagiarism and other types of dishonest behavior. Their results sup- ported the original idea that the fraudulence is all in their head"," the supposed impostors reported on a survey that they cheated less often than control subjects who did not have impostor feelings. Verbaldel ELF16A V1.indd 14\""]'::jsonb, 'C', '2016vt_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2016vt', 2, 35),
