('What are we told about recessions?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Sustainability Question We can talk all we like about recycling and sustainable agri- 31. What is implied here? culture, but population is the issue that really matters. Yet it is the one on which so many people are silent. We have A Recycling has been found to be the best way to made the human right to reproduce unchallengeable: to do achieve a sustainable society. so is either to be eugenicist or – as with China’s one-child B There is an urgent need to set up global popu policy – repressively authoritarian. But sooner or later we lation targets. have to do something. No matter how much recycling we C More funding has now been allocated to mod do, how much renewable energy we create and how much ernize the food industry. better we become at producing food, there has to come a D There is a risk of a world collapse unless more time when the world’s population makes the planet unsus- effort is put into energy production. tainable. The pressure on resources is being maintained at both ends of the spectrum: not only are more babies being born, people are living longer and longer. Recession Question Economists usually try to solve problems by tinkering 32. What are we told about recessions? with things, and making them more complicated. That is because it often works – until, suddenly, it doesn’t. The A Confident professionals seem to be less affected Nobel Prize winner Paul Krugman, who is a specialist by them than those who are less sure. in recessions, takes us through the history of why they B They involve both risk-taking and gambling with happen. It is always because people devise an ingenious other people’s money. way to make what appears to be free money and nobody C They are related to lack of trust and are one of understands the consequences until it is too late. There the ways in which capitalism works. is, it turns out, no such thing as a free lunch. The entire D They are a result of people’s unethical drive to edifice of capitalism is based on capital – which is really make more money than they need. just another word for confidence. If people believe your confidence to be authentic, the risk you take is likely to be small. But as soon as people think you are bluffing, they panic – and panic destroys wealth faster than confidence can ever increase it. Verbaldel ELF16A V1.indd 11 2016-01-07 13:27:46 New Orleans When Hurricane Katrina hit New Orleans in late August asters and an almost constant sense of flux. Partly this was 2005, the city and its people suffered wounds that are still a result of its location. Or, as New Orleans Mayor Marc healing. While the rebuilding efforts continue apace, every Morial put it in 2001: ‘This was a lousy place to put a city.’ one seems to agree that the city that eventually emerges Unsurprisingly, therefore, the city’s particular relationship from the wreckage will be very different to the “old” New with catastrophe has long been proverbial. Orleans. But it was not just the physical city that was As bad as hurricanes and conflagrations and potential devastated by Katrina. Equally damaged was its carefully slave rebellions could be, perhaps no single threat was cultivated image as the city that care forgot. Shocked by more pressing and more characteristic of 19th-century the indelible images of destructio', 'd28608502767', '["Confident professionals seem to be less affected by them than those who are less sure.","They involve both risk-taking and gambling with other people’s money.","They are related to lack of trust and are one of the ways in which capitalism works.","\"They are a result of people’s unethical drive to make more money than they need. 2016-01-07 13:27:46 asters and an almost constant sense of flux. Partly this was a result of its location. Or, as New Orleans Mayor Marc Morial put it in 2001: ‘This was a lousy place to put a city.’ Unsurprisingly, therefore, the city’s particular relationship with catastrophe has long been proverbial. As bad as hurricanes and conflagrations and potential slave rebellions could be, perhaps no single threat was more pressing and more characteristic of 19th-century New Orleans than disease. In the years before the Civil War, New Orleans had the highest mortality rate in the United States. Cholera carried away nearly 5,000 people in 1832 alone. More terrifying still were the periodic visita- tions of yellow fever. The city suffered multiple outbreaks of the disease from the late 18th to the early 20th century, but it was in the middle of the 19th century that the worst incidents took place. In countless ways, the previous traumas in the life of New Orleans prefigured the ways in which the evils of Katrina and its aftermath unfolded. Indeed, what the events of 2005 also revealed was the degree to which, even before Katrina, this was a city marked by disaster. While 21st-century New Orleans may not have been bothered by repeated yellow fever epidemics, it was certainly plagued by the highest per-capita murder rate in America – an accolade, unfortunately, that the city currently looks set on reclaiming. Yet, if this modern moment of devastation evokes past disasters, it also brings to mind previous moments of rebirth for the city. The continued existence of New Orleans is a story of survival against the odds and repeated renewals. Of course, this new beginning is tinged with tragedy. That there have already been profound problems with the rebuilding effort is clear for all to see. That those problems have been unfairly spread is equally evident"," a recent study showed that the city’s neighbourhoods are now more segregated than they were before Katrina. The seemingly inevitable cycles of death and rebirth might be woven into the fabric of New Orleans’ history and, as an incident like the BP oil spill highlighted, there is little doubt that a city in such a ‘lousy place’ is likely to face hard times again. But as the process of rebuilding continues, this might at least be the moment to lay some ghosts to rest. Thomas Ruys Smith, History Today 2016-01-07 13:27:46\""]'::jsonb, 'C', '2016vt_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2016vt', 2, 32),
