('What is implied about the Shona people in the first two paragraphs?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. How to Get Good Grades Alternatives Education is the handmaiden of economic growth: teach future work- 31. ers well, it is argued, and they will go on to invigorate the economy. No A doubt 31_____, then, that the OECD, a club of mostly rich countries, goes to B problem great lengths to discover how the school systems in its member countries C surprise are doing. Every three years, the so-called PISA study will detail and rank D harm the reading, mathematics and science skills of 15-year-olds in each country. But even more important than ranking school systems is knowing how to 32. make them better. That is the aim of a recent study by McKinsey, a con- A supported sultancy firm. For starters, McKinsey says, throwing money at education does not seem to do much good, at least in those countries that already send all their young people to school. America, for example, 32_____ its spend- 33. ing on schools by 21% between 2000 and 2007, while Britain pumped in 37% more funds. Yet in this period, according to PISA, standards in both countries slipped. Many school systems that were not showered with extra funds did much better. D improvers What separates the big spenders from the 33_____, McKinsey found, is the awareness that different types of school system respond to radically 34. different types of reform. In countries where schools mainly seek to teach A once pupils to read, write and grasp some basic maths, centralisation seems to B since work. All teachers should be directed to teach the same lessons from the C though same textbooks. However, 34_____ the school system can teach to basic D before standards, it should pay more attention to collecting detailed data on exami- nation results. This serves not just to make schools accountable, but helps 35. to identify the best teaching methods. A transferred Countries where schools have already attained a higher standard should B covered become pickier in choosing teachers. Another study by McKinsey in 2007 C replaced concluded that making teaching a high-status profession was what 35_____ D boosted standards. For instance, schools could recruit teachers from among the best university graduates. The Economist Verbaldel-ELF16A del2.indd 11 2016-01-07 13:23:34 Shona Sculpture In Zimbabwe, the Shona people were traditionally known The muvesi created household items with great artistry as “the People of the Mist”, since they inhabited the mist- in wood and clay, though not usually stone, as a way of shrouded Inyanga Mountains, from whose stone their recounting what he had seen in a dream. This heritage is descendants have been creating extraordinary sculpture embedded in the work of modern Shona sculptors, though over the past 55 years or so. theirs is clearly not for functional purposes. The Shona are the oldest and also the largest ethnic The present art movement surged into public con- group in the country, and the legendary guardians of sciousness in the early 1960s with the encouragement of King Solomon’s mines. Despite the fact that many of Frank McEwen, the first director of the National Gallery them are now urbanised, the essence of their misty land in Harare. He quickly realised the inherent sculptural bril- and its spiritual world is deep within their psyche and l', '237e9031e62a', '["The majority of them now work and live in the big cities of Zimbabwe.","They used to make a living as miners in ancient times.","Their brilliant sculpture is a relatively modern phenomenon.","Few of them can now remember their old way of life."]'::jsonb, 'C', '2016vt_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2016vt', 4, 36),
