('What does the text imply about the writer’s attitude to SheTaxis? The writer …', 'ELF', 'verbal', '"Women-only Cabs Questions Women have been driving yellow cabs in New York since 31. What are we told in relation to SheTaxis? the 1940s, but 99% of drivers are male. Even among drivers of cars booked by phone or online, only 4% are women. A It is about time women should be given a chance That may change with the launch of SheTaxis, an app to drive yellow cabs. that lets female passengers insist on female drivers, and B Its drivers have been treated with scepticism vice versa. by their male colleagues. But SheTaxis faces two speed bumps. One is practi- C It is doubtful whether male passengers will cal. Demand has been so great that the firm has had to accept female cab drivers. decelerate its launch until it can recruit 500 drivers. The D Its business idea has met with unexpected other obstacle is legal. By employing only female drivers, success among passengers. SheTaxis is obviously discriminating against men. Since anti-discrimination law is not always applied with com- 32. What does the text imply about the mon sense, that may be illegal. writer’s attitude to SheTaxis? The writer … New York’s female drivers. the spirit of the law. majority of cab drivers. the new taxi company. Religion and Well-being religious individuals, people who lack a creed are less likely pared with 88 percent in Mississippi. In the states where to be happy and healthy – surely the two most important religion was very important, people were much more likely earthly concerns – and tend to lose out on at least seven to be living in difficult circumstances. They also had lower years of life, some estimates suggest. Several large-scale subjective well-being than people living in less religious population studies have reinforced a single premise: the parts of the country. Did religion make them happier, as more you engage with religious activities, the better off previous studies had shown? Absolutely, according to the you are. data – but they were worse off than the contented resi- Yet many people are less certain about their beliefs than dents of more affluent states, where religion mattered less. ever before. Non-believers number between 500 million Globally, people in 154 countries were surveyed, with and 750 million worldwide, according to one analysis. In an average of 3,000 people per country. Overall, three out the past two decades, the percentage of the U.S. popula- of four reported that religion was important in their life. tion that proclaims its religious affiliation as “none” has Again, the devil was in the details. The countries in more than doubled, to about 15 percent. Many of these the sample varied in religiosity by a factor of six, ranging individuals do not identify as atheists', '9bdabc66615b', '[" even so, many of from 16 percent of respondents in Sweden agreeing that them conduct their life outside the religious establish- religion was an important part of their life to 99 percent ment. Although research on this population is only now affirmation in Bangladesh, Egypt, Sri Lanka and Somali- emerging, a recent study found that people who were less land. In this global poll, a tough environment also tended committed to their religious creed were actually less happy to coincide with greater national religiosity. If you live in than avowed atheists. a nation where daily existence is difficult, your life satis-\"","is concerned about current prejudice against New York’s female drivers.","seems to think that the firm’s policy conforms to the spirit of the law.","is upset about its discriminating effect on the majority of cab drivers.","believes that the law will be changed in favour of the new taxi company. 2017-06-15 13:03:38 in Vermont, 44 percent rated religion as important, com- pared with 88 percent in Mississippi. In the states where religion was very important, people were much more likely to be living in difficult circumstances. They also had lower subjective well-being than people living in less religious parts of the country. Did religion make them happier, as previous studies had shown? Absolutely, according to the data – but they were worse off than the contented resi- dents of more affluent states, where religion mattered less. Globally, people in 154 countries were surveyed, with an average of 3,000 people per country. Overall, three out of four reported that religion was important in their life. Again, the devil was in the details. The countries in the sample varied in religiosity by a factor of six, ranging from 16 percent of respondents in Sweden agreeing that religion was an important part of their life to 99 percent affirmation in Bangladesh, Egypt, Sri Lanka and Somali- land. In this global poll, a tough environment also tended to coincide with greater national religiosity. If you live in a nation where daily existence is difficult, your life satis- faction is generally lower. In those countries, being more religious appears to grant you a premium on happiness that your less religious neighbours do not enjoy. If the living is easy, however, both nonbelievers and religious people have similar, relatively high subjective well-being. This effect held true for all religions represented in the sample – Buddhism, Christianity, Hinduism and Islam. More important, whether a person was struggling or thriving was nowhere near as predictive of religiosity as a society’s conditions and norms. “Do you have individual choice about what you believe? Yes, but there are strong influences in society – whether everyone around you is religious,” Ed Diener says. “That’s the eye-opener for me, that societal forces led to religiosity rather than individual forces.” For nonbelievers, these are heartening data. They bol- ster the idea that believers will accrue more psychological benefits only in places that value religion more, and vice versa. It means atheists are not permanently shut off from some fountain of happiness – although they may want to find a like-minded community to live in. Sandra Upson, Scientific American Mind 2017-06-15 13:03:38 2017-06-15 13:03:38"]'::jsonb, NULL, '2017ht_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2017ht', 1, 32),
