('What is the most accurate description of women’s role in the British Foreign Office in the period between the two world wars?', 'ELF', 'verbal', '"In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Is Science Real? Alternatives Are you ever 31_____ to ask whether entities such as electrons, black holes 31. and the Higgs particle really exist? As a chemist I worry about what is real A expected and dependable in my field. I also care because all of this goes to the heart B persuaded of an old, important – and unresolved – debate about how to regard scien- C supposed tific discoveries. D tempted There are two main camps in this debate: scientific realism and anti- realism. Scientific realism holds that if science has made great progress by 32. invoking entities such as electrons, then we should take the next step of A speculative accepting that they really do exist, that the world described by science is the “real” world. Our present theories are too 32_____ to have happened by chance: somehow we have latched onto the blueprint of the universe. This is not to everybody’s taste. Anti-realists accept the progress made by 33. science but stop short of making the additional leap of 33_____ of believing in the materiality of things they cannot actually see. The anti-realist typically presents a counter-argument along these lines: so many past theories have come and gone, why should we ever regard any of them as real? D relevance Anti-realists also argue that their approach places them in a better posi- tion to 34_____ change when an established entity or theory becomes 34. redundant. Not investing belief in a particular theory, they claim, allows A fend off them to move on to alternatives more easily. B adapt to Realists retort such an approach is cavalier, or even dangerous. Science C step up progresses by creeping up on the truth about the world: 35_____ succes- D account for sive theories were merely to replace one another, that progress would be truly miraculous. 35. Eric Scerri, New Scientist B as Opening the Doors of Diplomacy In her interwar classic, Three Guineas, Virginia Woolf In Vansittart’s words: ‘It is a false argument to say that, imagined the following scene. The daughter of an edu- because women are treated as equals in this country, an cated man, in the course of conversation with a brother Englishwoman abroad will be so treated by foreigners. She or male acquaintance, raises the possibility that newly will not.’ The Ambassador to Berlin, Sir Eric Phipps, rein- enfranchised women should now be admitted to all pro- forced the point, noting that Nazi officials ‘would prob- fessions, including those still reserved for men: ably feel that they themselves were not being taken suf- We on our side of the table become aware at once of some ficiently seriously for being asked to receive her’. ‘strong emotion’ on your side ‘arising from some motive below The second argument centred on the rough and tumble the level of conscious thought’…', 'cdb41029354a', '[" a glance at the private psy- of ports, where consular officers could be called upon to chometer shows that the emotional temperature has risen from placate violent and inebriated sailors, deal with outbreaks ten to twenty degrees above normal. of venereal disease on merchant ships, or investigate alle- Woolf was not present at the proceedings of the depart- gations of ‘homosexual cri\"","They were generally allowed to work in offices in Britain but rarely abroad.","They worked with background tasks but were not permitted to perform any public duties.","They were usually not allowed to be involved in any type of work in the Foreign Office.","They played active roles in diplomatic negotia- tions but this was not officially recognized."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2017ht_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2017ht', 4, 36),
