e long and loud. Setting Standards In the long list of problems that plague American edu- Now, after years of failure, the push for common standards cation, one is primary: what should students learn? For has new life. NCLB’s reforms were plainly insufficient. decades, however, this question has baffled people. In an A chorus of wonks and professors say that bad schools education system run by the 50 states, success is in the eye will undermine America’s competitiveness in future. Most of the beholder. Mississippi has different expectations for important, the states, in the shape of the governors and pupils than Massachusetts does. America as a whole has chief school officers, are leading the effort. Recently the fallen behind. In a ranking of 15-year-olds in 30 industrial- NGA and the CCSSO published a report calling for ised countries in 2006, American teenagers came a dismal reform and now all states except Texas and Alaska have 21st in science and 25th in maths. signed on, agreeing to support their plan. Now there is a new drive to set national standards. The The NGA and CCSSO hope to create standards for US Secretary of Education has offered more than $4 bil- maths and English that are clear, focused and rigorous. lion in total of federal money to states that pursue certain Dane Linn, the NGA’s education director, explains that reforms – in particular, adopting standards and assess- the drafters have tried to foll', 'f48ecb028a29', '["People living in India rarely need to know more than one language.","Knowing two languages keeps an individual mentally alert for a longer period of time.","For people diagnosed with dementia, higher education leads to a longer life.","The more languages you know, the better your mental health will remain late in life."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2017vt_provpass-3-verbal-a-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2017vt', 3, 31),
