 be long and loud. Setting Standards In the long list of problems that plague American edu- Now, after years of failure, the push for common standards cation, one is primary: what should students learn? For has new life. NCLB’s reforms were plainly insufficient. decades, however, this question has baffled people. In an A chorus of wonks and professors say that bad schools education system run by the 50 states, success is in the eye will undermine America’s competitiveness in future. Most of the beholder. Mississippi has different expectations for important, the states, in the shape of the governors and pupils than Massachusetts does. America as a whole has chief school officers, are leading the effort. Recently the fallen behind. In a ranking of 15-year-olds in 30 industrial- NGA and the CCSSO published a report calling for ised countries in 2006, American teenagers came a dismal reform and now all states except Texas and Alaska have 21st in science and 25th in maths. signed on, agreeing to support their plan. Now there is a new drive to set national standards. The The NGA and CCSSO hope to create standards for US Secretary of Education has offered more than $4 bil- maths and English that are clear, focused and rigorous. lion in total of federal money to states that pursue certain Dane Linn, the NGA’s education director, explains that reforms – in particular, adopting standards and assess- the drafters have tried to foll', 'f48ecb028a29', '["chorus of wonks and professors say that bad schools will undermine America’s competitiveness in future. Most important, the states, in the shape of the governors and chief school officers, are leading the effort. Recently the NGA and the CCSSO published a report calling for reform and now all states except Texas and Alaska have signed on, agreeing to support their plan. The NGA and CCSSO hope to create standards for maths and English that are clear, focused and rigorous. Dane Linn, the NGA’s education director, explains that the drafters have tried to follow the example of high-per- forming countries such as Singapore and states such as Massachusetts. The first task is to provide standards for what high-school graduates should know to succeed in college or the workforce. The draft guidelines for English include examples of suitable content, such as Jane Austen’s Pride and Prejudice and a lecture by Toni Morrison. The second task is to create standards for each grade. Mr Linn hopes to present final standards in the near future. There are many difficulties ahead. The standards may turn out to be too lax. Some states may reject them. States that do adopt the standards will need to revise curricula, textbooks and the way they train teachers, explains Ches- ter Finn of the Fordham Institute, a conservative think- tank. New standards demand new tests. The stimulus offered provides $350 million to develop common assess- ments, but these may not be ready for years. In the end, however, American students will at least know where they stand, and where they should be going. The Economist 2017-01-16 10:39:58 2017-01-16 10:39:58","Industry has by now accepted Carson’s main arguments.","The basic idea of Silent Spring remains controversial.","Carson was wrong in many of her environmental predictions. 2017-01-16 10:39:58 Now, after years of failure, the push for common standards has new life. NCLB’s reforms were plainly insufficient."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2017vt_provpass-3-verbal-a-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2017vt', 3, 32),
