nce in IQ. On the negative side, first-borns are the only ones who experience having 35. their parents all to themselves, then having to share them. For this reason, A for they are thought to be anxious, emotionally intense, defensive and 34_____ B although jealous rages. Another characteristic is caution and aversion to risk. C so Many theorists group only children amongst first-borns – 35_____ they D because never experience the frictions, fights or fondness that comes with siblings. Anna Moore, Psychologies Magazine Globalisation Globalisation is commonly seen as a distinctly modern but this focus allows Mann to explore the ramifications of state of the world, resulting from air travel and free trade. actions in a more detailed way. In 1493, Charles Mann shows this is not so – we have been Colonisation of the Americas has previously been con- interconnected for a very long time indeed. trasted by degree of exploitation – the English went to cul- Our perception of a local and pristine bygone world is tivate and the Spanish to extract. But Mann convincingly in fact an imagined version of the past. Trade and exchange paints a picture of extraction by all European colonists, have linked Europe and the Americas since Europeans first whether exhausting the land along the eastern seaboard sailed beyond their shores. In this wonderfully entertain- of what is now the US by planting tobacco or by mining ing and subtly balanced book, Mann traces some of these silver in the Bolivian Andes. linkages, telling familiar stories mixed with fascinating Mann al', '7618226011ee', '["Widespread historical notions have little connec tion with reality.","Modern international relations are less extensive than often believed.","Commercial incentives are not enough to build an interconnected world.","Today’s globalisation can be seen as little else than a popular myth."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2018vt_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2018vt', 2, 36),
