ears- us beyond the formula of technical limitations plus emo- al. ‘I’m telling you,’ she says, ‘me and my old voice, it just tional sincerity that has governed so much discussion of go up a little bit and come down a little bit. It’s not legit.’ her music, and Szwed pays Holiday a high compliment And she was right. With a vocal range barely stretching by focusing less on her authenticity than on her artistry. beyond an octave, she really could only go up and down Her powers of rhythmic and melodic invention were leg- ‘a little bit’, and she wouldn’t have lasted a day at any of endary among the musicians she played with. Her spon- the conservatories that trained aspiring singers in the ‘le- taneous adaptations of well-known tunes, often necessi- git’ techniques of classical or operatic performance. Since tated by her need to compress a song’s range, were them- Holiday was incapable of the vocal gymnastics displayed selves improvisations of the highest standard, sometimes by the likes of Sarah Vaughan and Ella Fitzgerald, her producing entirely new melodies. place in the first rank of jazz singers surely rests on oth- Then there was her way with rhythm. ‘Lagging’, or er qualities. It’s in drawing those out that John Szwed’s playing behind the beat, was already a common device book is at its most valuable. of jazz musicians when Holiday began recording in the The familiar view has it that what imbued Holiday’s\"","Her lasting fame in jazz circles remains a mystery to this day.","Her special way of singing sets her apart from other female performers.","Her technical limitations were obviously a mat ter of pride to her.","Her abilities as a jazz singer are severely ques tioned by Szwed."]'::jsonb, 'B', '2019vt_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2019vt', 4, 36),
