I’m telling you,’ she says, ‘me and my old voice, it just tional sincerity that has governed so much discussion of go up a little bit and come down a little bit. It’s not legit.’ her music, and Szwed pays Holiday a high compliment And she was right. With a vocal range barely stretching by focusing less on her authenticity than on her artistry. beyond an octave, she really could only go up and down Her powers of rhythmic and melodic invention were leg- ‘a little bit’, and she wouldn’t have lasted a day at any of endary among the musicians she played with. Her spon- the conservatories that trained aspiring singers in the ‘le- taneous adaptations of well-known tunes, often necessi- git’ techniques of classical or operatic performance. Since tated by her need to compress a song’s range, were them- Holiday was incapable of the vocal gymnastics displayed selves improvisations of the highest standard, sometimes by the likes of Sarah Vaughan and Ella Fitzgerald, her producing entirely new melodies. place in the first rank of jazz singers surely rests on oth- Then there was her way with rhythm. ‘Lagging’, or er qualities. It’s in drawing those out that John Szwed’s playing behind the beat, was already a common device book is at its most valuable. of jazz musicians when Holiday began recording in the The familiar view has it that what imbued Holiday’s\"","emerged","declined","vanished","increased"]'::jsonb, 'D', '2019vt_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2019vt', 4, 34),
('', 'ELF', 'verbal', '"In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Exercise Alternatives Loneliness is not good for long-distance runners, or 31. anyone else who exercises regularly. That’s the conclu- A improve sion suggested by an experiment showing that animals B move 31 better with stress hormones released by physical C adjust activity if they have company when they exercise. D cope Although exercise speeds the production of new brain cells, it also raises the level of the stress hormone corti- 32. costerone, which by itself has 32 effect. Since social contact helps to reduce stress in many animals, includ- ing humans, Elizabeth Gould and fellow psychologists at Princeton University asked whether social contact affects neuron development in rats after they exercise. Some of the rats were housed in groups of three', 'c81cfd705587', '[" the rest were housed alone. Half of each group were allowed 33. a daily 33 on an exercise wheel, while the others got A ration no exercise. B workout The researchers measured the levels of corticosterone C portion in the rats’ blood twice a day, and after 12 days they mea- D rundown sured neuronal development in the hippocampus, the seat of learning and memory in the brain. After exercise, 34. corticosterone levels 34 by the same amount in the A emerged rats that had company as in the isolated rats. However, B declined cell growth in the brains of the group that exercised was C vanished faster than in the sedentary rats, and fastest in the rats D increased that exercised and had company. According to Gould, this 35 the idea that social sup- 35. port helps to lessen the negative consequences of stress. New Scientist Lady Sings the Blues conversation with some fellow musicians at a rehears- us beyond the formula of technical limitations plus emo- al. ‘I’m telling you,’ she says, ‘me and my old voice, it just tional sincerity that has governed so much discussion of go up a little bit and come down a little bit. It’s not legit.’ her music, and Szwed pays Holiday a high compliment And she was right. With a vocal range barely stretching by focusing less on her authenticity than on her artistry. beyond an octave, she really could only go up and down Her powers of rhythmic and melodic invention were leg- ‘a little bit’, and she wouldn’t have lasted a day at any of endary among the musicians she played with. Her spon- the conservatories that trained aspiring singers in the ‘le- taneous adaptations of well-known tunes, often necessi- git’ techniques of classical or operatic performance. Since tated by her need to compress a song’s range, were them- Holiday was incapable of the vocal gymnastics displayed selves improvisations of the highest standard, sometimes by the likes of Sarah Vaughan and Ella Fitzgerald, her producing entirely new melodies. place in the first rank of jazz singers surely rests on oth- Then there was her way with rhythm. ‘Lagging’, or er qualities. It’s in drawing those out that John Szwed’s playing behind the beat, was already a common device book is at its most valuable. of jazz musicians when Holiday began recording in the The familiar view has it that what imbued Holiday’s\"","obscures","determines","reinforces","disproves To think of Holiday as having ‘technique’ already takes us beyond the formula of technical limitations plus emo- tional sincerity that has governed so much discussion of her music, and Szwed pays Holiday a high compliment by focusing less on her authenticity than on her artistry. Her powers of rhythmic and melodic invention were leg- endary among the musicians she played with. Her spon- taneous adaptations of well-known tunes, often necessi- tated by her need to compress a song’s range, were them- selves improvisations of the highest standard, sometimes producing entirely new melodies. Then there was her way with rhythm. ‘Lagging’, or playing behind the beat, was already a common device of jazz musicians when Holiday began recording in the 1930s, but the exceptionally pronounced delay she often used took her early accompanists by surprise. No less innovative was her approach to accenting and phrasing lyrics, often placing the stress in unexpected places and, Szwed suggests, adding an almost abstract dimension to words by employing them not only in their representa- tional sense but also, through accentuation and modifi- cation, as pure sound that conveyed its own meanings. Emotion was indeed a hallmark of her singing, but it was through these sharply honed techniques and imaginative leaps that emotion was wrought into beautiful music. This is a curious book, though. Billed as a biography on its dust jacket, in reality it’s anything but. Szwed feels obliged to tackle the ‘myth’ of Holiday in the first, and shorter, of the book’s two sections, but these chapters have a rather ragbag character, with little sense of nar- rative progression. It’s not at all clear why we’re given a vignette about the party antics of Woolworth heir Jimmy Donahue, or potted biographies of various other periph- eral figures, when we’re told scarcely the basics about Holiday’s early years. It’s only when Szwed moves on to the ‘musician’ that the book comes into its own – though even then, what fascinates him seems to be less the mu- sician than the voice. Daniel Matlin, Literary Review"]'::jsonb, 'C', '2019vt_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2019vt', 4, 35),
