('What is claimed in the first paragraph?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Twins and Religion Question In a 2005 study, researchers analyzed reports on the re- 31. What is the main point here? ligiosity of twins in adolescence compared with adult- hood. The intent was to calculate, by means of a statis- A Genetic and environmental influences are equally important for people’s religious tical model, the relative importance of genetic factors attitudes. versus environmental influence at those two stages of life. For adolescents, they learned that genetics – in oth- B Personality factors are largely irrelevant to er words, dispositions for certain personality traits – grown-ups’ religious faith. accounted for only 12 percent of their religious identity, C Different factors determine people’s views on and a shared upbringing contributed 56 percent. (If you religion during the course of their lives. include a third category, which captures all the unique events that shape a twin’s life, these three numbers add beliefs over time. up to 100 percent.) Conversely, 44 percent of adults’ re- ligiosity could be attributed to genetics, and 18 percent had to do with their environment. Rove Beetles Question It’s quite a ploy. Rove beetles blend seamlessly into army 32. What is implied here? ant societies, but instead of helping out, they devour the young of their unsuspecting companions. The deceit is A The way in which various beetles develop is basically impossible to predict. so successful that it has evolved independently in at least 12 parasitic rove beetle species – a phenomenon called B It is likely that ants and rove beetles will convergent evolution. In each case, the beetles’ entire eventually blend into one and the same species. body shape has evolved to resemble the army ants they C The emergence of certain beetles appears to prey on, and they smell and act like the ants too. The violate most evolutionary principles. finding challenges arguments that different creatures would evolve if the evolutionary clock was started from nature of evolutionary development. scratch. Instead, it suggests that evolution may take si- milar and predictable paths whenever a certain scena- rio arises. Time Humans, like creatures ranging from amoebas to ele- tial metaphors they use can vary. Take the future-in- phants, come with built-in equipment for perceiving some front metaphor, found in English and many other lan- aspects of time, such as the rhythms of night and day, and guages (as in “the week ahead of us”). This metaphor the turning of the seasons. What separates humans from was long thought to be universal, but in 2006 a striking other animals is that we dice it into units, even ones that counterexample was encountered in South America. In go beyond what is perceivable, such as milliseconds, or Aymara, a language spoken in the Andes, many phrases that transcend our life span, such as millennia. In short, actually suggest the opposite metaphor. For example, the humans everywhere create and rely on time concepts – expression “a long time ago” could be loosely rendered in ideas about the nature of time. Aymara as “a lot of time in front.” But what are these time concepts? What is going on in For the Aymara, knowledge acquired through vis- the mind of, e.g., a speaker of Yupno, high in the moun- ual perception is taken to be certain and reliable. It is tains of Papua New Guinea, or English for that matter, of utmost importance to communicate facts and stories when answerin', '571162aee5e0', '["Humans and other animals apparently share the same notion of time.","Some animal species are more dependent on time than others for survival.","All animals are genetically programmed to sense the passage of time.","Evolutionary pressure explains the significance of time for all animals."]'::jsonb, 'C', '2020ht_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2020ht', 2, 33),
