 if the evolutionary clock was started from nature of evolutionary development. scratch. Instead, it suggests that evolution may take si- milar and predictable paths whenever a certain scena- rio arises. Time Humans, like creatures ranging from amoebas to ele- tial metaphors they use can vary. Take the future-in- phants, come with built-in equipment for perceiving some front metaphor, found in English and many other lan- aspects of time, such as the rhythms of night and day, and guages (as in “the week ahead of us”). This metaphor the turning of the seasons. What separates humans from was long thought to be universal, but in 2006 a striking other animals is that we dice it into units, even ones that counterexample was encountered in South America. In go beyond what is perceivable, such as milliseconds, or Aymara, a language spoken in the Andes, many phrases that transcend our life span, such as millennia. In short, actually suggest the opposite metaphor. For example, the humans everywhere create and rely on time concepts – expression “a long time ago” could be loosely rendered in ideas about the nature of time. Aymara as “a lot of time in front.” But what are these time concepts? What is going on in For the Aymara, knowledge acquired through vis- the mind of, e.g., a speaker of Yupno, high in the moun- ual perception is taken to be certain and reliable. It is tains of Papua New Guinea, or English for that matter, of utmost importance to communicate facts and stories when answerin', '571162aee5e0', '["He was the first to focus on time from a human perspective.","He argued that all languages use spatial meta phors to express time.","He pioneered research into Native American languages.","\"He stressed the strong influence of linguistic patterns on thought. Meat Appetite for meat is growing as the developing world becomes more prosperous. But meat – especially beef – can be polarizing, on health, environmental and ethi- cal grounds. Chicken outpaced beef in the United States in 2010. Per capita beef consumption in the U.S. peaked in 1976 at 91.5 pounds a year"," it has since fallen more than 40 percent. Argentina’s famous appetite for beef has fallen because of cholesterol consciousness and eco- nomic downturns. In countries where meat is a newly affordable option, animal protein is a boon, not a debate. Dalits The National Dalit Memorial and Green Garden, which opened recently, stands out. It is dedicated to the his- torically oppressed Dalit community and was commis- sioned by one of the few Dalit women to rise to power in India: the former chief minister of the state of Uttar Pradesh, Mayawati. Dalit is a modern term for a wide cluster of social groups who were historically the lowest of India’s castes – shunned, marginalized and even con- sidered a source of contamination. Historically, being a Dalit – it has replaced the ugly word “untouchable” – trig- gered social practices intended to segregate and exclude. Though caste-based discrimination is illegal under the Indian Constitution, the social and legal reality is far more complex. Awe Through a representative national survey, a study led by researcher Paul Piff ascertained that people who report feeling awe more often are, in fact, more gener- ous. When given raffle tickets and offered the chance to donate some, those who frequently felt awe gave away more tickets. Then the researchers conducted four exper- iments in which they induced awe in some participants and other emotions such as pride or amusement in oth- ers. They evoked awe through videos of breathtaking natural scenes and by taking subjects outside to gaze upward at towering eucalyptus trees. In every case, those who experienced awe behaved in what psycholo- gists call a more “prosocial” way, being more helpful or making more ethical decisions. By making us feel like a small part of something grander, the authors suggest, awe shifts our attention from our own needs to those of the greater good.\""]'::jsonb, 'D', '2020ht_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2020ht', 2, 35),
