('', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Two Drinks a Day? Alternatives mon type of stroke by about a tenth. However, any more A enhances than that 31 the risk of the most deadly strokes, B modifies according to a summary of 27 studies involving more C decreases than 20,000 patients. D improves Researchers believe that alcohol could hamper the for- mation of dangerous blood clots. But they caution against 32. drinking to protect the brain, saying that a rise in blood pressure could offset any benefit. The study adds to the 32 around the health benefits of light drinking. ska Institutet in Sweden, suggests a benefit in protect- ing against ischemic strokes, which make up the bulk of 33. cases, resulting from a blockage in the blood 33 to the brain. People were 8 per cent less likely to suffer an A track ischemic stroke if they had one or two drinks a day. B pressure Susanna Larsson, lead author of the paper, says: “Pre- C drain vious research has found an association between alcohol D supply consumption and lower levels of fibrinogen – a protein in the body which helps the formation of blood clots. While 34. this may explain the connection between light to moder- A beneficial ate alcohol consumption and lower ischemic stroke risk, B neutral the 34 effect of alcohol consumption on blood pres- C adverse sure – a major risk factor for stroke – may increase the D modest risk of another type of stroke.” About 15 per cent of strokes are the result of blood 35. vessels bursting within the brain. This type, known as haemorrhagic, tends to be fatal. No 35 has so far been found between light drinking and haemorrhagic stroke. However, people who had more than four drinks a day D trace were 67–82 per cent more likely to suffer one. Shamim Quadir, of the Stroke Association, says: “This research suggests that there is much more to understand about the effects of alcohol consumption on the different types of stroke.” Chris Smyth, The Times Schools in Britain British grammar schools are state-supported secondary If there is evidence that grammar schools help entrench schools that select their pupils through an exam taken at inequality, there is, however, little evidence that phas- age 11, known as the 11-plus. Once a center-piece of Brit- ing them out has helped improve social mobility. A land- ain’s education system, they have largely been phased out mark 2005 study from the London School of Economics, over recent decades. which described social mobility in Britain as “low and The history of grammar schools goes back to the Mid- falling,” showed that two children born, respectively, dle Ages, but the modern version emerged out of the 1944 into poor and prosperous families in 1958 were more Education Act, one of a series of laws that shaped social equal as adults than two similar children born in 1970. policy in postwar Britain. It introduced free education up More recently, a 2010 report from the OECD found that to the age of 15, and set up three kinds of schools: gram- on social mobility Britain came near the bottom of the mar, technical and secondary moderns. class among rich nations. Few technical schools were ever built, so children The g', '7773c5dd1c73', '["evidence","link","resemblance","trace If there is evidence that grammar schools help entrench inequality, there is, however, little evidence that phas- ing them out has helped improve social mobility. A land- mark 2005 study from the London School of Economics, which described social mobility in Britain as “low and falling,” showed that two children born, respectively, into poor and prosperous families in 1958 were more equal as adults than two similar children born in 1970. More recently, a 2010 report from the OECD found that on social mobility Britain came near the bottom of the class among rich nations. The geography of social mobility has also changed. A recent government report observed that the key division now is between London, where even children from dis- advantaged backgrounds do relatively well, and coastal and old industrial towns in the north and east of Eng- land, where most of what the report terms “social mobil- ity coldspots” are to be found. This division maps onto a larger, deepening political fault line. The fact that neither selective nor nonselective school systems have improved social mobility in Britain might suggest that the problem lies in the very idea of using schools to engineer a more equal society. A decent educa- tion system can help a few individuals progress beyond the circumstances of their birth, but is unlikely to change fundamentally the social and economic structures that entrench inequality and restrain social mobility. In focusing on social mobility, what has gone miss- ing is the idea of education as a good in itself. One of the reasons people regard grammar schools with nostalgia is that they seem to represent a standard of good educa- tion. But they do so for only a few. At the heart of selective schooling is the assumption that pupils from disadvantaged backgrounds are better off getting “vocational” training rather than being intel- lectually challenged. The trouble is, that sentiment has persisted in the nonselective schools, too. The result is that Britain has ended up with a state system in which every child receives an equally mediocre education. Kenan Malik, International New York Times"]'::jsonb, 'B', '2020ht_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2020ht', 4, 35),
