m We all know the stories of the Brothers Grimm. We’ve Indeed, parents are some of the primary figures of dan- seen them brought to life in films, cartoons and plays, but ger in the Brothers Grimm fairy tales. Interestingly, the true Grimms’ tales are pure folklore, passed down while later versions took steps to (slightly) tone down from generation to generation and, like all the best sto- the horror with wicked stepmothers, the earliest ver- ries, they were spoken. sions feature biological parents taking the most drastic, It was that oral tradition of storytelling that first drew terrifying measures. Take Hansel and Gretel, for example. the brothers to these classics. Jacob and Wilhelm were These days, it’s the woodcutter’s new wife who is largely tremendously proud of their national heritage, and col- responsible for abandoning the children in the woods, lecting fairy tales was an opportunity to preserve and with the husband a reluctant and remorseful accomplice celebrate a popular German tradition. who later comes back for his children. In the earliest ver- The enduring image of the Grimms is of two travel- sion, it is the unlucky siblings’ own mother who propels ling scholars, journeying far and wide across the land to this evil plan, and their father is just as complicit. Simi- track down these stories and record them for posterity. larly, we all think of the wicked queen in Snow White as The truth, of course, is somewhat different.', '79ef3075e56b', '["A large number of their early stories did not turn up in their later volumes.","The quality of the writing increased as new versions appeared.","All the now famous stories are included in the first two volumes.","The originality of their storytelling was imme diately apparent."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2021vta_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2021vta', 1, 34),
('What are we told about the content in the different versions of the fairy tales?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Neuroscience Question In recent decades, neuroscience has emerged as a star 31. What is said about the academic discipline of among the biological disciplines. But its enormous pop- neuroscience? ularity as an academic career choice has been accompa- nied by a drop in the percentage of trained neuroscien- the field. tists who actually work in academic research positions – largely because of lack of funding. Steven Hyman of the B There is strong competition among applicants to Broad Institute of the MIT and Harvard University wel- the graduate courses. comes the flood of doctoral students choosing the field C There are many students who never finish their but warns: ‘Insofar as talented young people are discour- academic studies. aged from academic careers by funding levels so low that they produce debilitating levels of competition or simply number of graduates. foreclose opportunities, the U.S. and the world are losing an incredibly precious resource.’ Animal Hacks Question Humans aren’t the only creatures to use tools. Archerfish 32. What is the main point here? shoot water droplets from their mouths to fell insects. Octopuses carry coconut shells to serve as shelter. Oran- A The ability to use tools can’t be directly connected to greater mental capacity. gutans borrow canoes to forage for aquatic plants. “Tool use is widespread and diverse,” says biologist Robert W. B Human tool use might be older in origin than Shumaker. But it’s not necessarily a sign of intelligence. many researchers have assumed. For some animals, like the archerfish, tool use is mostly C Relatively advanced thinking appears to be a instinctive: each individual of the species does it, in the requirement for the ability to use tools. same way. Other animals learn their skills: before the canoeing orangutans ventured out on the water, they indicated by previous research. observed how humans used the craft. The Brothers Grimm We all know the stories of the Brothers Grimm. We’ve Indeed, parents are some of the primary figures of dan- seen them brought to life in films, cartoons and plays, but ger in the Brothers Grimm fairy tales. Interestingly, the true Grimms’ tales are pure folklore, passed down while later versions took steps to (slightly) tone down from generation to generation and, like all the best sto- the horror with wicked stepmothers, the earliest ver- ries, they were spoken. sions feature biological parents taking the most drastic, It was that oral tradition of storytelling that first drew terrifying measures. Take Hansel and Gretel, for example. the brothers to these classics. Jacob and Wilhelm were These days, it’s the woodcutter’s new wife who is largely tremendously proud of their national heritage, and col- responsible for abandoning the children in the woods, lecting fairy tales was an opportunity to preserve and with the husband a reluctant and remorseful accomplice celebrate a popular German tradition. who later comes back for his children. In the earliest ver- The enduring image of the Grimms is of two travel- sion, it is the unlucky siblings’ own mother who propels ling scholars, journeying far and wide across the land to this evil plan, and their father is just as complicit. Simi- track down these stories and record them for posterity. larly, we all think of the wicked queen in Snow White as The truth, of course, is somewhat different.', '79ef3075e56b', '["In later versions, the horror of the early editions is simply removed.","In early versions, mothers are consistently put in much less favourable light than fathers.","In later versions, the evildoers have changed into people less close to the children.","In early versions, parents are often just as much victims as the children. Somatic Feedback Ask average folks how emotions work – about the causal relationship between feelings and behavior – and they’ll say we smile because we’re happy, we run because we’re afraid. But work by psychologists shows the truth is often the reverse: what we feel is actually the product, not the cause, of what we do. It’s called “somatic feedback.” Only after we act do we deduce, by seeing what we just did, how we feel. However, new research suggests there may be limits to our ability to proactively manage our own well-being. As expected, across the experiments, the researchers found that the more people smiled, the hap- pier they reported being. But only some people. Surprisingly, for a section of the population, smil- ing actually reduced well-being. The more these people smiled, the less happy they were. Who are these people for whom extra smiling fails to generate corresponding increases in joy? In the answer lies the ultimate irony. It turns out that the gloomiest people were those who believed in precisely that somatic feedback hypothesis described above. People who realized, in other words, that you can “smile to feel happy” (called proactive smil- ers) were exactly those who did not enjoy the benefits of the theory they espoused. On the other hand, for those who believed that smiling is a genuine indicator of mood – those who subscribed to commonsense notions about the causal order of action and emotion (reactive smilers) – smiling boosted happiness. Science Fiction Science-fiction writers are no better than anyone else at predicting what is to come. The future, after all, is unknowable. Often people try to extrapolate a recent trend straight into the future, but society and technol- ogy rarely change at a constant rate. Other foretelling techniques are similarly doomed to failure. Science fic- tion, however, does recognize the value of prognosticat- ing: it tends to reveal the preoccupations of the present."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2021vta_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2021vta', 1, 35),
