thers Grimm We all know the stories of the Brothers Grimm. We’ve Indeed, parents are some of the primary figures of dan- seen them brought to life in films, cartoons and plays, but ger in the Brothers Grimm fairy tales. Interestingly, the true Grimms’ tales are pure folklore, passed down while later versions took steps to (slightly) tone down from generation to generation and, like all the best sto- the horror with wicked stepmothers, the earliest ver- ries, they were spoken. sions feature biological parents taking the most drastic, It was that oral tradition of storytelling that first drew terrifying measures. Take Hansel and Gretel, for example. the brothers to these classics. Jacob and Wilhelm were These days, it’s the woodcutter’s new wife who is largely tremendously proud of their national heritage, and col- responsible for abandoning the children in the woods, lecting fairy tales was an opportunity to preserve and with the husband a reluctant and remorseful accomplice celebrate a popular German tradition. who later comes back for his children. In the earliest ver- The enduring image of the Grimms is of two travel- sion, it is the unlucky siblings’ own mother who propels ling scholars, journeying far and wide across the land to this evil plan, and their father is just as complicit. Simi- track down these stories and record them for posterity. larly, we all think of the wicked queen in Snow White as The truth, of course, is somewhat different.', '79ef3075e56b', '["There is a serious lack of qualified teachers in the field.","There is strong competition among applicants to the graduate courses.","There are many students who never finish their academic studies.","There are few research positions to match the number of graduates."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2021vta_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2021vta', 1, 31),
('What is the main point here?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Neuroscience Question In recent decades, neuroscience has emerged as a star 31. What is said about the academic discipline of among the biological disciplines. But its enormous pop- neuroscience? ularity as an academic career choice has been accompa- nied by a drop in the percentage of trained neuroscien- the field. tists who actually work in academic research positions – largely because of lack of funding. Steven Hyman of the B There is strong competition among applicants to Broad Institute of the MIT and Harvard University wel- the graduate courses. comes the flood of doctoral students choosing the field C There are many students who never finish their but warns: ‘Insofar as talented young people are discour- academic studies. aged from academic careers by funding levels so low that they produce debilitating levels of competition or simply number of graduates. foreclose opportunities, the U.S. and the world are losing an incredibly precious resource.’ Animal Hacks Question Humans aren’t the only creatures to use tools. Archerfish 32. What is the main point here? shoot water droplets from their mouths to fell insects. Octopuses carry coconut shells to serve as shelter. Oran- A The ability to use tools can’t be directly connected to greater mental capacity. gutans borrow canoes to forage for aquatic plants. “Tool use is widespread and diverse,” says biologist Robert W. B Human tool use might be older in origin than Shumaker. But it’s not necessarily a sign of intelligence. many researchers have assumed. For some animals, like the archerfish, tool use is mostly C Relatively advanced thinking appears to be a instinctive: each individual of the species does it, in the requirement for the ability to use tools. same way. Other animals learn their skills: before the canoeing orangutans ventured out on the water, they indicated by previous research. observed how humans used the craft. The Brothers Grimm We all know the stories of the Brothers Grimm. We’ve Indeed, parents are some of the primary figures of dan- seen them brought to life in films, cartoons and plays, but ger in the Brothers Grimm fairy tales. Interestingly, the true Grimms’ tales are pure folklore, passed down while later versions took steps to (slightly) tone down from generation to generation and, like all the best sto- the horror with wicked stepmothers, the earliest ver- ries, they were spoken. sions feature biological parents taking the most drastic, It was that oral tradition of storytelling that first drew terrifying measures. Take Hansel and Gretel, for example. the brothers to these classics. Jacob and Wilhelm were These days, it’s the woodcutter’s new wife who is largely tremendously proud of their national heritage, and col- responsible for abandoning the children in the woods, lecting fairy tales was an opportunity to preserve and with the husband a reluctant and remorseful accomplice celebrate a popular German tradition. who later comes back for his children. In the earliest ver- The enduring image of the Grimms is of two travel- sion, it is the unlucky siblings’ own mother who propels ling scholars, journeying far and wide across the land to this evil plan, and their father is just as complicit. Simi- track down these stories and record them for posterity. larly, we all think of the wicked queen in Snow White as The truth, of course, is somewhat different.', '79ef3075e56b', '["The ability to use tools can’t be directly connected to greater mental capacity.","Human tool use might be older in origin than many researchers have assumed.","Relatively advanced thinking appears to be a requirement for the ability to use tools.","Animal tool use is much more common than indicated by previous research. Indeed, parents are some of the primary figures of dan- ger in the Brothers Grimm fairy tales. Interestingly, while later versions took steps to (slightly) tone down the horror with wicked stepmothers, the earliest ver- sions feature biological parents taking the most drastic, terrifying measures. Take Hansel and Gretel, for example. These days, it’s the woodcutter’s new wife who is largely responsible for abandoning the children in the woods, with the husband a reluctant and remorseful accomplice who later comes back for his children. In the earliest ver- sion, it is the unlucky siblings’ own mother who propels this evil plan, and their father is just as complicit. Simi- larly, we all think of the wicked queen in Snow White as being an evil interloper into a happy family, but in the original, the monster who orders the Huntsman to kill Snow White is actually the maiden’s own mother. Brutal events and punishments abound in the rest of the collection. “There is a strong sense of social justice in almost all of the tales,” says Zipes. “And despite some violence, most of the tales end on a happy note. The dif- ficulty is that most people do not read all 210 tales of the final edition to see how diverse, and to a certain extent, how humorous the tales are. The notion that the Grimms’ tales are dark is derived from the ignorance of critics, teachers and parents.” It has been noted that a possible reason for the recur- ring villainy of women and the tragic absence of father figures is a reflection of the Grimms’ own loss of their father and grandfather. Whether they made those alter- ations themselves or not, the themes of famine found throughout these stories are certainly a reflection of the times in which they originated. In subsequent editions, the Grimms began to edit these stories to make them more suitable for their younger readers, as a reflection of their enormous success, and began adding more Chris- tian elements. The themes of punishment are present right from the start and the villains’ deeds are certainly wicked, but the reprisals are often more severe. Jonathan Hatfull, All About History"]'::jsonb, NULL, '2021vta_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2021vta', 1, 32),
