('What are we told in the first paragraph concern ing the giant panda’s new protection status?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Australian Prehistory Alternatives Researchers Patrick Nunn and Nicholas Reid believe that a 31. number of indigenous stories from across the Australian con- A erroneously tinent 31 record events between 18,000 and 7,000 years B faithfully ago, when the sea rose 120 m. Reid said a key feature of indig- C barely enous storytelling culture – a distinctive “cross-generational D visually cross-checking process” – might explain the remarkable con- sistency in accounts passed down by preliterate people which 32. researchers previously believed could not persist for more than 800 years. Scholars of oral traditions have previously been sceptical of how accurately they reflect real events. However, Nunn and Reid argue that the stories provide empirical corroboration of a postglacial sea level rise documented by marine geographers. 33. Some of the stories are 32 factual accounts, such as those around Port Phillip Bay near Melbourne, which tell of the loss A surely of kangaroo hunting grounds. Others, especially older stories B then such as those from around Spencer Gulf in South Australia, are C possibly allegorical: an ancestral being angered by the misbehaviour of D not a clan punishes them by taking their country, gouging a groove with a magical kangaroo bone for the sea to swallow up the land. 34. “Our sense originally was that the sea level must have been A massive creeping up very slowly and that this would 33 have been B unexpected noticeable in an individual’s lifetime,” Reid said. “But we’ve C modest come to realise through conducting this research that Australia D tolerable must in fact have been abuzz with news about this. There must have been constant inland movement, reestablishing relation- 35. ships, negotiating with inland neighbours about encroaching onto their territory,” Reid said. “There would have been 34 consequences of this.” The fortunes of those faced with the decision to retreat as D neglected camps, tracks and dreaming places were slowly swallowed up – especially on islands – were mixed. Those on Rottnest and Kan- garoo Islands cut and ran up to 7,000 years ago. Others, such as those at Flinders Island in Bass Strait, made the fateful deci- sion to stay, and died out as the land grew arid and fresh water became scarce. Reid said that while it was impossible to prove that the indigenous oral tradition had continued unbroken over time, its contemporary features gave a clue as to why it may be the world’s most faithful and 35 . The Guardian Giant Pandas Good news about conservation is always welcome, and being hopeless at reproduction – females come into heat never more so than when it concerns that most char- only a couple of days a year. But the old difficulty of get- ismatic of charismatic mega-fauna, the giant panda. In ting them to breed in captivity owed at least as much to 2016, the International Union for the Conservation of human ignorance. As knowledge of panda biology and Nature (IUCN), an environmental group that links gov- behaviour has soared, so has the birth rate. ernments and non-governmental organisations (NGOs), Unfortunately, this is making no difference to the downgraded the international symbol of cuteness from wild population. The point of the captive breeding pro- “endangered” (meaning with a high risk of extinction in gramme is to repopulate the wild. Pandas born in cap-', '627c67a8a0b9', '["It had been expected long before it was finally announced.","It is due to a decision made by an influential organisation.","It has given rise to heated debate among environmentalists.","It was decided on mainly by Chinese conservationists."]'::jsonb, NULL, '2021vta_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2021vta', 4, 36),
