th inland neighbours about encroaching onto their territory,” Reid said. “There would have been 34 consequences of this.” The fortunes of those faced with the decision to retreat as D neglected camps, tracks and dreaming places were slowly swallowed up – especially on islands – were mixed. Those on Rottnest and Kan- garoo Islands cut and ran up to 7,000 years ago. Others, such as those at Flinders Island in Bass Strait, made the fateful deci- sion to stay, and died out as the land grew arid and fresh water became scarce. Reid said that while it was impossible to prove that the indigenous oral tradition had continued unbroken over time, its contemporary features gave a clue as to why it may be the world’s most faithful and 35 . The Guardian Giant Pandas Good news about conservation is always welcome, and being hopeless at reproduction – females come into heat never more so than when it concerns that most char- only a couple of days a year. But the old difficulty of get- ismatic of charismatic mega-fauna, the giant panda. In ting them to breed in captivity owed at least as much to 2016, the International Union for the Conservation of human ignorance. As knowledge of panda biology and Nature (IUCN), an environmental group that links gov- behaviour has soared, so has the birth rate. ernments and non-governmental organisations (NGOs), Unfortunately, this is making no difference to the downgraded the international symbol of cuteness from wild population. The point of the captive breeding pro- “endangered” (meaning with a high risk of extinction in gramme is to repopulate the wild. Pandas born in cap-', '627c67a8a0b9', '["erroneously","faithfully","barely","visually"]'::jsonb, NULL, '2021vta_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2021vta', 4, 31),
