. No Child Left Undiagnosed Unless we soon come to our senses, the United States permission to advertise directly to consumers, a practice may become a dystopia of overdiagnosed mental illness, allowed only in the United States and New Zealand. The starting with early childhood. Already, one in five adults results? A sector delivering annual sales close to $10 bil- takes psychotropic medication on a daily basis. One in lion – and thousands of kids taking medicine they prob- four of us has qualified for a psychiatric diagnosis in the ably don’t need. last year, and half of us will have been diagnosed with a Some child psychologists have now proposed a wholly mental disorder at some point in our lifetime. new condition, closely related to ADHD, that could poten- The statistics for children and teenagers are even tially subject millions more children, especially younger more alarming: a recent study found that by age 21, 81 kids, to reckless diagnosis and treatment. “Sluggish cog- percent of American kids could be labeled with a mental nitive tempo” (SCT) may be the most ill-informed inven- disorder – if they are evaluated often enough and with tion I have encountered in my 40 years of evaluating sufficient diagnostic ardor. diagnostic wish lists. In the past 20 years, the rate of attention deficit hyper- To date, SCT’s main characteristics are vaguely activity disorder (ADHD) diagnoses has tripled: 11 per- described and still under discussion, but include', 'c4ed70f4f133', '["It is no longer seen as a mental disorder among teenagers and children.","Its overall frequency is believed to have increased by nearly a third.","It now appears to have reached almost epidemic proportions.","Its spread among young people is mainly attributed to better diagnostic tools."]'::jsonb, 'C', '2023ht_provpass-3-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2023ht', 3, 34),
('What is argued in relation to today’s treatment of ADHD?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'American Folk Music Question The national treasure that is American folk and country 31. What is said here about the relationship music came over on boats from the British Isles in the between British and American folk songs? 17th century, especially the Scots-Irish borderlands. It traveled down the Appalachian – via fiddle, banjo and were replaced. guitar – in ballads and hymns, the words changing into new songs but the music immutable. For example, it B The original British folk songs developed beyond was the first music that the great American songwriter recognition. Woody Guthrie ever heard: his mother singing Anglo- C American folk music gradually lost touch with its Celtic ballads in a high nasal country twang at their home British roots. outside Okemah, Oklahoma. with new music. The Great Lakes Question The Great Lakes are undergoing “an ecological catas- 32. What is implied here? trophe unlike any this continent has seen,” according to Pulitzer Prize finalist Dan Egan. Humans have dramati- A Measures against environmental destruction tend to be taken when it is already too late. cally altered the lakes’ fauna since invasive species first snuck up through the man-made Saint Lawrence Seaway. B Environmental damage caused by chemical Blunders sometimes stemmed from well-meaning poli- waste is usually very difficult to repair. cies. Researchers imported Asian carp to kill river nui- C The chemical industry is unlikely to take sances without chemicals, and now some worry the fish responsibility for effects on the environment. has silently invaded Lake Michigan’s floor via the Chi- cago Sanitary and Ship Canal. And the lakes’ imported protect the environment can be difficult. problems are quickly becoming national disasters, such as the tiny and quick-spawning quagga mussel that has infested regions as far away as Lake Mead and Lake Pow- ell on the Colorado River. No Child Left Undiagnosed Unless we soon come to our senses, the United States permission to advertise directly to consumers, a practice may become a dystopia of overdiagnosed mental illness, allowed only in the United States and New Zealand. The starting with early childhood. Already, one in five adults results? A sector delivering annual sales close to $10 bil- takes psychotropic medication on a daily basis. One in lion – and thousands of kids taking medicine they prob- four of us has qualified for a psychiatric diagnosis in the ably don’t need. last year, and half of us will have been diagnosed with a Some child psychologists have now proposed a wholly mental disorder at some point in our lifetime. new condition, closely related to ADHD, that could poten- The statistics for children and teenagers are even tially subject millions more children, especially younger more alarming: a recent study found that by age 21, 81 kids, to reckless diagnosis and treatment. “Sluggish cog- percent of American kids could be labeled with a mental nitive tempo” (SCT) may be the most ill-informed inven- disorder – if they are evaluated often enough and with tion I have encountered in my 40 years of evaluating sufficient diagnostic ardor. diagnostic wish lists. In the past 20 years, the rate of attention deficit hyper- To date, SCT’s main characteristics are vaguely activity disorder (ADHD) diagnoses has tripled: 11 per- described and still under discussion, but include', 'c4ed70f4f133', '["Its use of special medication is completely uncalled for.","It is beneficial only if based on an accurate diagnosis.","Its assumed positive effects remain to be demonstrated.","It may give rise to the mental disorder it was meant to cure. Tantalum The name of element 73 in the periodic table is derived from the ancient Greek mythological character Tantalus, whose punishment for sacrificing his son was to stand in a lake in the afterworld, surrounded by water but una- ble to drink. Swedish chemist Anders Ekeberg chose the name after he discovered the element tantalum in 1802, because tantalum’s oxide would not react with acids, even plentiful amounts of very strong acids, unlike the compounds of most other metals. The inert nature of tan- talum makes it ideal for certain medical implants, includ- ing screws used to hold together bones, artificial joints and plates in the skull. Combined with its strength, this inertness makes it ideal in making vessels and pipes in the pharmaceutical and food industries. Forget It? According to Viktor Mayer-Schönberger, professor of Internet governance and regulation at the Oxford Inter- net Institute, the ability to forget past events, or at least to let them recede in our minds, is critical for decision mak- ing. Psychologists often note that this ability is a valuable safety valve. As we naturally forget things over time, we can move on and make future choices without difficult or embarrassing episodes clouding our outlook. But when our decisions are tangled up in the perfect memory of the Internet – when we must factor in the effect of our online footprint before every new step – “we may lose a fundamental human capacity to live and act firmly in the present,” Mayer-Schönberger says. El Greco The 16th- and 17th-century paintings of El Greco are popu- lated by famously elongated figures. These curious forms have kindled speculation that the painter may have suf- fered from astigmatism, an optical defect. The reasoning goes that spectacle lenses could have overcorrected El Greco’s astigmatism, producing retinal images that were stretched vertically, thus causing the master to paint tall and skinny objects that appeared normal to him. To test this idea, a vision scientist persuaded a volunteer to wear a distorted telescope for two days straight. She cop- ied squares and drew squares from memory four times each day. The copied squares were always picture-per- fect, but the squares from memory were not always so: they started 50 percent too tall and grew progressively shorter with time. By the end of the second day, she was drawing impeccable squares. The conclusion was that even if El Greco suffered from astigmatism, he would have quickly adapted to it. – 1"]'::jsonb, 'B', '2023ht_provpass-3-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2023ht', 3, 35),
