('What is the purpose of the first paragraph?', 'ELF', 'verbal', '"In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Itch Alternatives The sensation of itch, which shares many nervous pathways 31. with pain, has long been a mystery. One reason is that it can be A turn conjured up entirely by the brain. Just thinking about something B let tickly, or even watching someone else scratch, can 31 us off. C put Efforts to find out why this is, and why only scratching makes D set it stop, have led researchers to study how itch is processed in the skin, spinal cord and brain. Itch, like chronic pain, is a surprisingly common problem. It comes as a side effect of opioid painkillers, the use of which 32. has rocketed in recent years. It also comes with skin condi- tions, such as eczema, as well as nerve damage, kidney disease and allergies. An estimated one in five of us will experience chronic itch at some point, and current remedies are provid- ing little 32 . The most common treatments are antihistamines, which block the part of the immune reaction that causes itching when 33. we are bitten by a bug, for example. Not all itches are immune- related, however, and antihistamines have proven to be 33 for A unproblematic most forms of chronic itch. B superfluous In recent years, researchers have discovered new kinds of C ineffective receptors in the skin and spinal cord that play a role in the sen- D sufficient sation of itch. They are working to figure out what naturally activates these receptors in the hope of 34 drugs that can 34. deactivate them. A improving Further clues on how to tackle itch come from brain imaging B revealing studies. Many brain areas activated by pain are also active dur- C enhancing ing itch, but one area that seems distinctively responsive to itch D designing is the precuneus. This brain area is involved in visual process- ing and memory, says Gil Yosipovich at the University of Miami. 35. “We can’t pinpoint what it does in itch, but it’s 35 activated A uniquely with itch as opposed to pain,” he says. B rarely For now, though, the single most effective way to get rid of C consequently an itch is what we do instinctively: to scratch. D hardly Stephani Sutherland, New Scientist Lothar and Theutberga It is a familiar story: a king dissatisfied with a loveless Waldrada – who some suspected had bewitched him, marriage that had lost its political value, burning to such was his emotional attachment to her. marry another woman accused by some of witchcraft', 'ce095ade6d8e', '[" But in handing the case over to the bishops, the young a group of educated royal advisers frantically devis- king had committed an error which ultimately brought ing strategies to make their king’s wishes a reality"," an down his kingdom. It was effectively an invitation for intransigent pope refusing to countenance any remar- others to get involved. The rulers of the kingdoms on riage on principle, with a dash of realpolitik thrown in. either side of Lothar’s realm were his two older uncles, The king is Lothar II, great-grandson of Charlemagne"," also descendants of the great Charlemagne – and with the the two women Theutberga and Waldrada"," the pope dream of reuniting Charlemagne’s fragmented empire Nicholas I. This was not just a royal divorce scandal – never very far from their thoughts. The king\"","To illustrate the difference between the past and the present."]'::jsonb, 'C', '2023ht_provpass-5-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2023ht', 5, 36),
