 done society a great 35 by 35. putting important issues on the agenda. But the most sensible conclusion to draw is that we ought to eat fewer, better-reared A justice animal products. Arguably, in my view the future belongs not B deal to vegetarians and vegans but to discerning “flexitarians” and C service conscientious omnivores. D cause Julian Baggini, New Statesman No Smiling Matter An endless stream of new discoveries makes science thrill- Together with a team of colleagues, he worked with a ing. But, as any seasoned researcher knows, such novel- sample based on the one used in the original study. All ties are worthless unless they can be replicated. Often, of the players in that were included, along with a larger, though, replication does not get done as thoroughly as it non-overlapping set of 527 Baseball-Register images of should be – or even at all. For, as any seasoned researcher players who were active slightly before, or after, 1952. also knows, replication is rarely the stuff careers are built Just like the researchers in the first experiment, Dr on', 'a44f728f41c3', '[" studies conducted with that goal may even struggle Dufner relied on “blind” volunteers to rate the intensity to get published in peer-reviewed journals. of the smiles in the images. Of the pictures he worked In this context, a recent attempted replication is impor- with, 40% showed no smile, 42% showed a partial smile tant, for it actually was published recently in a journal and 18% showed a full smile. called Psycholog\"","Scientific findings must be reproducible under similar conditions to be considered valid.","Many researchers seem to be ignorant of con ventional scientific wisdom.","Repeated experimentation is even more crucial now than in the dawn of scientific research.","Today’s science is becoming increasingly open to elementary criticism."]'::jsonb, 'A', '2023vt_provpass-5-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2023vt', 5, 36),
