g known as much. Yet science began prob- phenomenon. ing the depth of this facility only a few years ago. Moominland and Beyond Moominvalley, with its snow-covered mountains, lush in the Gulf of Finland. The truth was she stayed in her greenery and friendly, peace-loving creatures, seems as remote summer home with her partner Tuulikki Pie- far as one can get from the horrors of war. But the Moom- tilä, a graphic artist and professor whom she had met ins have more to do with strife and struggle than is imme- in 1955, but the women’s relationship was often glossed diately obvious. Their creator, Tove Jansson (1914–2001), over. Being gay was illegal in Finland until 1971, and was a political cartoonist from the age of 15, skewer- only declassified as an illness in 1981. Jansson herself, ing fascism and communism during the Second World however, defied labels, according to her niece Sophia: War. She channelled the wartime pain of her native Fin- “It wasn’t gender that mattered to Tove, it was the land into her apocalyptic first book, The Moomins and the individual.” Great Flood (1945), a reminder that however calm things While her paintings were well received in Finland, an may seem on the surface, life can be both frightening and influential critic concluded that they would fail to make unpredictable. an impression internationally. This must have left Jans- In Dulwich Picture Gallery’s retrospective of Jansson’s son demoralised as she struggled to balance her time work, th\"","Its superficial focus on war trauma is a reflection of her personal experiences.","Its key message is promoting international peace and understanding.","There is a tension between first impressions and the underlying realities.","The idyllic setting is intended as an escape from the tragedy of war."]'::jsonb, 'C', '2024ht_provpass-3-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2024ht', 3, 33),
