non. ing the depth of this facility only a few years ago. Moominland and Beyond Moominvalley, with its snow-covered mountains, lush in the Gulf of Finland. The truth was she stayed in her greenery and friendly, peace-loving creatures, seems as remote summer home with her partner Tuulikki Pie- far as one can get from the horrors of war. But the Moom- tilä, a graphic artist and professor whom she had met ins have more to do with strife and struggle than is imme- in 1955, but the women’s relationship was often glossed diately obvious. Their creator, Tove Jansson (1914–2001), over. Being gay was illegal in Finland until 1971, and was a political cartoonist from the age of 15, skewer- only declassified as an illness in 1981. Jansson herself, ing fascism and communism during the Second World however, defied labels, according to her niece Sophia: War. She channelled the wartime pain of her native Fin- “It wasn’t gender that mattered to Tove, it was the land into her apocalyptic first book, The Moomins and the individual.” Great Flood (1945), a reminder that however calm things While her paintings were well received in Finland, an may seem on the surface, life can be both frightening and influential critic concluded that they would fail to make unpredictable. an impression internationally. This must have left Jans- In Dulwich Picture Gallery’s retrospective of Jansson’s son demoralised as she struggled to balance her time work, th\"","Researchers have long taken an interest in dogs’ ability to learn from people.","Most animals have been observed to imitate human behaviour.","Sheep dogs seem to be especially good at copy ing their owners’ behaviour.","\"Animals learning from people is a relatively rare phenomenon. the Gulf of Finland. The truth was she stayed in her mote summer home with her partner Tuulikki Pie- lä, a graphic artist and professor whom she had met 1955, but the women’s relationship was often glossed er. Being gay was illegal in Finland until 1971, and ly declassified as an illness in 1981. Jansson herself, wever, defied labels, according to her niece Sophia: t wasn’t gender that mattered to Tove, it was the dividual.” While her paintings were well received in Finland, an fluential critic concluded that they would fail to make impression internationally. This must have left Jans- n demoralised as she struggled to balance her time tween different projects. She provided the illustra- ons for the Swedish-language editions of Lewis Carroll’s ice’s Adventures in Wonderland and JRR Tolkien’s The bbit. Although these differ in style from the Moomin oks, her signature is clearly there – in the ominous ndscapes, dark caves and bright moonlit skies. Jansson’s admiration for strong women is clear. One of r paintings, Print Maker (1975), depicts Pietilä hard at rk in her studio, a proud artist surrounded by her cre- ions. Jansson’s last self-portrait, painted at 64, shows r with a pained face and her eyes ringed with red, but e look of controlled defiance is still there. The Moomin oks, too, feature plenty of complicated female char- ters, from the calm and gentle Moominmamma to the iky Little My – who resembles Jansson herself. She also id homage to the real women in her life: Pietilä appears a character called Too-Ticky, while the inseparable ingumy and Bob represent Jansson and Vivica Bandler, married theatre director whom she met and fell in love th in 1946. At the age of 80, Jansson said that while her life had en colourful, she would do everything differently ven another chance. A world without Moomins, though, uld be a meaner place"]'::jsonb, 'D', '2024ht_provpass-3-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2024ht', 3, 32);
