('What is the main point in the first paragraph with regard to the study of humour?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Daylight Saving Time Question Ask Americans why they still turn their clocks back- 31. What is implied here in connection with DST in ward and forward twice a year, and they’ll likely cite the U.S.? the same reason: farmers said they needed more daylight in the field. This, however, is mostly a myth. While the early 20th century. farm lobby did participate in the policy debate a century ago, they were actually lobbying against daylight saving B Its introduction had a number of unforeseen time (DST). So who lobbied for DST if not the farmers? consequences. Industrialists and politicians, including President Wood- C It has received strong support from the energy row Wilson, who thought that increasing daylight hours lobby over the years. would decrease demand for electricity. Data remains mixed on whether DST does, in fact, reduce energy con- commercial profits. sumption to a significant degree. But it has succeeded in another one of its initial goals: getting more people to shop. That trend continues for many retailers today. Yawning Birds Question Many vertebrates yawn – but only humans, dogs, chimps 32. What, specifically, is new about the research and a species of rodent find yawns contagious. Now we reported? can add budgies to the list. Researchers placed budgies in adjacent cages, either with a barrier between them than previously thought. or with nothing obstructing their view. When the birds could see each other, they were around three times as B Apparently, showing signs of empathy is not likely to yawn within five minutes of a yawn from their exclusive to mammal species. neighbour. Videos of budgies yawning also caused them C Empathy is widespread among humans as well to follow suit. Contagious yawning is linked with empa- as most animals. thetic processes, according to researchers, so the find- ing suggests social non-mammals may have basic forms imitate yawning. of empathy. Something to Laugh About Perhaps it is the hours spent in archives or sipping tea alone does not explain the rampant popularity of jokes at conferences, but there can be few who value old jokes at the expense of the powerful. as much as historians do. The first efforts to recover the Along with the learned professions, politicians were humour of the past were made in the 1970s with the early (as ever) easy targets: those deemed unprincipled or self- stirrings of cultural history and, since then, it has become interested might find themselves likened to a weather- a well-trodden path to explore past mentalities. Laughter cock that turns its backside upon every wind, or a Thames is a fundamental human characteristic – the very thing waterman who looks one way but rows another. There that separates us from animals, if you follow Aristotle – were jokes about elites and their fashions: strutting fops but the subjects we see fit to laugh at change over time. so doused in orange-flower perfume that they had to The theory is simple: if you can ‘get’ the jokes of the past, keep up a swift pace for fear of suffocation. And jestbooks you can understand the interests and sensibilities of the contained countless tales of plucky underdogs outwitting people who inhabited it. a social superior. Penelope Corfield set out to do just this in her 1997 The frequency of such jokes was not lost on contem- article, ‘Laughing at the Learned’. Taking readers on a poraries and it prompted deep', '9f5df954046e', '["There is increasing evidence that the use of humour is a uniquely human trait.","In recent years, the way the Ancient Greeks explained humour has basically been refuted.","Throughout history and across regions, the targets of humour have been remarkably similar.","There are arguments that humour provides a gateway to the reasoning of former times."]'::jsonb, 'D', '2024vt_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2024vt', 1, 33),
