athy. Something to Laugh About Perhaps it is the hours spent in archives or sipping tea alone does not explain the rampant popularity of jokes at conferences, but there can be few who value old jokes at the expense of the powerful. as much as historians do. The first efforts to recover the Along with the learned professions, politicians were humour of the past were made in the 1970s with the early (as ever) easy targets: those deemed unprincipled or self- stirrings of cultural history and, since then, it has become interested might find themselves likened to a weather- a well-trodden path to explore past mentalities. Laughter cock that turns its backside upon every wind, or a Thames is a fundamental human characteristic – the very thing waterman who looks one way but rows another. There that separates us from animals, if you follow Aristotle – were jokes about elites and their fashions: strutting fops but the subjects we see fit to laugh at change over time. so doused in orange-flower perfume that they had to The theory is simple: if you can ‘get’ the jokes of the past, keep up a swift pace for fear of suffocation. And jestbooks you can understand the interests and sensibilities of the contained countless tales of plucky underdogs outwitting people who inhabited it. a social superior. Penelope Corfield set out to do just this in her 1997 The frequency of such jokes was not lost on contem- article, ‘Laughing at the Learned’. Taking readers on a poraries and it prompted deep', '9f5df954046e', '["They were mainly found among royalty and the established nobility.","They represented the same professions as in other periods.","They were basically people of high prestige in society.","They shifted dramatically from the learned to politicians and the clergy. Bone Fractures Antidepressants may be bad for your bones. People who take some selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors have been found to have a higher risk of fractures, but it wasn’t clear whether this was due to the drug or their depres- sion. Now researcher Patricia Ducy and colleagues have found that giving mice fluoxetine – the active ingredient in Prozac – for six weeks causes them to lose bone mass. The team identified a two-stage process by measuring bones, blood and gene activity. During the first three weeks, bones grew stronger as the fluoxetine impaired osteoclasts, cells that usually deplete bone tissue. But by six weeks, the higher levels of serotonin prompted by the drug disrupted the ability of the hypothalamus region of the brain to promote bone growth. Ducy says this two- phase pattern is also seen in people. Nevada Nevada has been described as ‘a laboratory for how Americans define freedom’. When settlers arrived, the land’s native peoples broadly comprised the Northern and Southern Paiute, Washoe and Western Shoshone peo- ple. In 1860, the Pyramid Lake War was fought between allied native forces and victorious US Army-backed settlers, who had been exerting their freedom by usurp- ing Paiute grazing land and disrupting food supplies. – White Elephants Rare, pale-skinned elephants were long considered sacred in Siam (modern-day Thailand), appearing on flags and symbolising royal power. Yet the pachyderm came at a price. Such was their prestige that they could not be used for work, and they required expensive food and specially made housing. According to legend, the kings of Siam presented a white elephant as a punish- ment-in-disguise to an unruly courtier. They had to accept the gift as a great honour, all the while knowing it would cost them a fortune. Although it is more likely that courtiers cared little for the cost, and it was actually foreign travellers who balked at the amount spent on an elephant, we still use the term to mean an expensive, use- less item that is more trouble than it’s worth."]'::jsonb, 'C', '2024vt_provpass-1-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2024vt', 1, 35),
