('What, according to the first two paragraphs, was Snow’s complaint in relation to “the two cultures”?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. The Illusion of Control Alternatives Modern surveillance does have some clear benefits. Cameras 31. in public spaces help the authorities detect crime and catch A though perpetrators, 31 they catch us in the dragnet as well. Cell B until phone tracking and networked cars allow us to be found if we C unless become lost or injured, and mapping apps are incredibly useful D since for directing us where we want to go. These features save lives – but all of them constantly transmit our location and generate a detailed record of our movements. Our social media history helps providers put the people and content we prefer front and 32. center when we log on, and our online searches allow marketers to offer us discounts at the places we shop most, all the while collecting data on our personal preferences and quirks. Given the difficulty of completely avoiding the 32 , it may be some- what reassuring to acknowledge this tradeoff. Laura Brandimarte of Carnegie Mellon University has studied people’s willingness to disclose personal information. She found 33. that when entities give people more control over the publication of their information, people disclose more about themselves – A censored even if it is also clear that the information will be 33 and B specified seen by others more often than it currently is. C outlined Brandimarte’s work demonstrates the concept of illusion of D accessed control. In many situations, we tend to 34 the control we have over events, especially when we get cues that our actions 34. matter. The risk to our private information comes not just from A ignore what we’ve shared but from how much of it is sold or made avail- B devalue able to others. And yet, when we feel that we have been given C reconsider more control over our information’s dissemination, our privacy D overestimate concerns decrease and our disclosure increases, even though that 35 control does not actually diminish the possibility 35. that our data will be shared. “The control people perceive over A intuitive the publication of personal information makes them pay less B apparent attention to the lack of control they have over access by oth- C deliberate ers,” Brandimarte says. D constant In other words, we are simply not very sophisticated when it comes to making choices about what to share. Jennifer Golbeck, Psychology Today The Two Cultures Few literary phrases have had such an enduring afterlife and a more measured one from Lionel Trilling. Snow’s as “the two cultures”, coined by C. P. Snow to describe cultural tribalism, Trilling argued, impaired the “possi- what he saw as a dangerous schism between scientists bility of rational discourse.” So why did Snow think the and literary intellectuals, i.e. between the sciences and supposed gulf between the two cultures was such a prob- the humanities. Yet nowadays few people actually seem lem? Because, in his view, it leads many capable minds to have read Snow’s book bearing that title. Why bother to ignore science as a vocation, which prevents us from when its main point seems so obvious? solving the world’s “main issue,” the wealth gap caused It was in 1959 that Snow, an English physicist, deliv- by industrialization, which threatens global stability. ered a lecture at Cambridge called “The T', '59342f3fd4cd', '["He worried about the lack of basic scientific knowledge among literary people.","He claimed that scientific progress was getting more than its fair share of attention in Western society.","He pointed to natural scientists’ patronizing attitude to literary culture in general.","He argued that different kinds of knowledge should be considered of