said. D issue However, Joe Grady, the general secretary of the University and College Union, said: “It is vital to recognise that education is 35. about much more than just financial benefit. Focusing on future income following university 35 the wider benefits that edu- cation brings to individuals and to society.” Richard Adams, The Guardian Cross-Cultural Psychology The academic discipline of psychology was developed information, such as the color of the water. Thus, the largely in North America and Europe. Some would argue holistic mode of thinking tends to focus on background that it has been remarkably successful in understand- and context just as much as foreground. This principle ing what drives human behavior and mental processes, accounts, too, for Masuda and Nisbett’s observation that which have long been thought to be universal. But in in a follow-up experiment, the Japanese participants “rec- recent decades some researchers have started question- ognized previously seen objects more accurately when ing this approach, arguing that many psychological phe- they saw them in their original settings rather than in nomena are shaped by the individual cultures in which novel settings, whereas this manipulation had relatively we live. little effect on Americans.” Experimental psychologists typically study behavior in Numerous studies have shown that cultural differ- a small group of people, with the assumption that any find- ences in thinking', '24c3d4e8f078', '["The scope and nature of psychological mecha nisms are being debated.","Long-established methods in the discipline of psychology have now been discarded.","Claims about the Western origin of psychology as a discipline are dubious.","Some psychological phenomena are not suited for scientific study."]'::jsonb, 'A', '2025ht_provpass-5-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2025ht', 5, 36),
