general secretary of the University and College Union, said: “It is vital to recognise that education is 35. about much more than just financial benefit. Focusing on future income following university 35 the wider benefits that edu- cation brings to individuals and to society.” Richard Adams, The Guardian Cross-Cultural Psychology The academic discipline of psychology was developed information, such as the color of the water. Thus, the largely in North America and Europe. Some would argue holistic mode of thinking tends to focus on background that it has been remarkably successful in understand- and context just as much as foreground. This principle ing what drives human behavior and mental processes, accounts, too, for Masuda and Nisbett’s observation that which have long been thought to be universal. But in in a follow-up experiment, the Japanese participants “rec- recent decades some researchers have started question- ognized previously seen objects more accurately when ing this approach, arguing that many psychological phe- they saw them in their original settings rather than in nomena are shaped by the individual cultures in which novel settings, whereas this manipulation had relatively we live. little effect on Americans.” Experimental psychologists typically study behavior in Numerous studies have shown that cultural differ- a small group of people, with the assumption that any find- ences in thinking', '24c3d4e8f078', '["ignores","confirms","rejects","includes information, such as the color of the water. Thus, the holistic mode of thinking tends to focus on background and context just as much as foreground. This principle accounts, too, for Masuda and Nisbett’s observation that in a follow-up experiment, the Japanese participants “rec- ognized previously seen objects more accurately when they saw them in their original settings rather than in novel settings, whereas this manipulation had relatively little effect on Americans.” Numerous studies have shown that cultural differ- ences in thinking styles are pervasive in cognition and that they affect memory, attention, perception, reason- ing, and even how we talk and think. For example, if you were asked to describe yourself, what would you say? Would you list your personal characteristics, such as being intelligent or funny, or would you use preferences, such as “I love pizza”? Or perhaps you would instead men- tion social relationships, such as “I am a parent”? Social psychologists have long maintained that people are much more likely to describe themselves and others in terms of stable personal characteristics than they are to describe themselves in terms of their preferences or relationships. However, in a landmark 1991 paper, social psycholo- gists Hazel R. Markus and Shinobu Kitayama put forward the idea that self-construal is culturally variant. They noted that individuals in some cultures understand the self as independent, a view more prevalent in Europe and North America, whereas those in other cultures perceive it as interdependent, a view more prevalent in Asia, Latin America, and Africa. Another domain that was originally dominated by studies of WEIRD samples is mental health. However, culture can affect our understanding of mental health in different ways. Because of the existence of cultural dif- ferences in behavior, the conceptual framework – based on detecting deviant or nonnormative behaviors – isn’t complete. What may be seen as normal in one culture (for example modesty) could be seen as deviating from the norm in another, and might be treated as a disorder or a social phobia. Clearly, culture has a massive effect on how we view ourselves and how we are perceived by others. Only by knowing about these effects will we ever be able to identify the core foundations of the human mind that we all share. Nicolas Geeraert, American Scientist"]'::jsonb, 'A', '2025ht_provpass-5-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2025ht', 5, 35);
