('What is implied about Florence Nightingale and nursing in the introductory paragraphs?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Pedro II Question Pedro II, emperor of Brazil, had already decided his 31. What is implied about Pedro II? throne would end with him when he was unexpectedly overthrown. On 15 November 1889, he was unseated by A His policy for freely accepting European immigrants was highly unpopular. a military coup, an inglorious end to an empire that had enjoyed new glory days under Pedro’s care. Brazil’s econ- B His rule led to a slow but consistent decline in omy grew, the arts scene flourished and the emperor Brazil’s general development. steered his country to victory in a string of international C His forced exile was a direct consequence of the disputes. He also oversaw the abolition of slavery (in way he treated his daughter. 1888) while his country became increasingly attractive to disgruntled Europeans who arrived there in waves, to his ability as a ruler. looking for a better life. However, forward-thinking Pedro remained traditional in one aspect and decided his sole heir, daughter Isabel, wouldn’t inherit his throne. When the military rose up and drove him into exile, Pedro put up little resistance. Within weeks, Brazil, once the heart of the Portuguese empire, had become a republic. Wildlife in Chernobyl Question Some 116,000 people fled the radioactive fallout from 32. What is the main point? the Chernobyl power plant when its reactor exploded in 1986, and another 220,000 were later resettled. In total, A It may be a long time before humans can safely return to the area. a zone covering around 4200 square kilometres split equally between Belarus and Ukraine was vacated. In a B Wildlife in the area seems to have recovered study of wildlife in the area by researcher Jim Smith, roe since the disaster. deer, elk, wild boar and wolves were surveyed between C Wild animals are less affected by radiation than 2008 and 2010 by counting tracks left in snow. Their humans are. abundance was compared with that seen between 2005 and 2010 in four uncontaminated nature reserves of sim- for wildlife. ilar size and habitat in Belarus. The density in Cherno- byl matched or exceeded those in other reserves. A sim- ilar picture was seen when wildlife numbers in Cher- nobyl were compared with those in the Bryansky For- est reserve 250 kilometres away in Russia. “Whatever negative effects there are from radiation, they are not as large as the negative effects of having people there,” says Smith. In the Footsteps of Florence Nightingale The World Health Organisation (WHO) designated 2020 What would disappoint Nightingale in her time-travel as the “Year of the Nurse”, marking 200 years since the to the present is that the transformation of nursing has birth of Florence Nightingale, who established the prin- been uneven. In countries as varied as India, Germany ciples of modern nursing and hospital sanitation. If she and Portugal, nurses are still largely treated as doctors’ were to drop in on a hospital today, Nightingale would be minions and may not even diagnose common ailments pleased to see the progress in nursing since her day – and or prescribe medication. And although nurses make up how it is poised to change in the years to come. nearly half of the world’s health-care workforce – and Nightingale founded the first nursing school, at a hos- 90% of patients’ contacts with health workers – they pital in London in 1860, and wrote some 200 books and are often not at the table when health-policy decisions papers. She was the first woman admitted to the Royal are', '94a7e2f7beda', '["She was guided by intuition rather than hard facts in her reform agenda.","Her once revolutionary ideas have long been common practice the world over.","She had to endure a lot of male prejudice in her use of statistical evidence.","Her reputation rests on her practical work rather than on her scientific insights."]'::jsonb, 'B', '2025vt_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-