th that seen between 2005 and 2010 in four uncontaminated nature reserves of sim- for wildlife. ilar size and habitat in Belarus. The density in Cherno- byl matched or exceeded those in other reserves. A sim- ilar picture was seen when wildlife numbers in Cher- nobyl were compared with those in the Bryansky For- est reserve 250 kilometres away in Russia. “Whatever negative effects there are from radiation, they are not as large as the negative effects of having people there,” says Smith. In the Footsteps of Florence Nightingale The World Health Organisation (WHO) designated 2020 What would disappoint Nightingale in her time-travel as the “Year of the Nurse”, marking 200 years since the to the present is that the transformation of nursing has birth of Florence Nightingale, who established the prin- been uneven. In countries as varied as India, Germany ciples of modern nursing and hospital sanitation. If she and Portugal, nurses are still largely treated as doctors’ were to drop in on a hospital today, Nightingale would be minions and may not even diagnose common ailments pleased to see the progress in nursing since her day – and or prescribe medication. And although nurses make up how it is poised to change in the years to come. nearly half of the world’s health-care workforce – and Nightingale founded the first nursing school, at a hos- 90% of patients’ contacts with health workers – they pital in London in 1860, and wrote some 200 books and are often not at the table when health-policy decisions papers. She was the first woman admitted to the Royal are', '94a7e2f7beda', '["It is still mainly focused on taking care of patients’ practical needs.","Patient safety will always depend on collabora tion between nurses and doctors.","There is an obvious risk that it will gradually lose its uniquely personal qualities.","It is increasingly blurring the old distinction between doctors and nurses."]'::jsonb, 'D', '2025vt_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2025vt', 2, 34),
('Which of the following statements is most in accordance with the text?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'Pedro II Question Pedro II, emperor of Brazil, had already decided his 31. What is implied about Pedro II? throne would end with him when he was unexpectedly overthrown. On 15 November 1889, he was unseated by A His policy for freely accepting European immigrants was highly unpopular. a military coup, an inglorious end to an empire that had enjoyed new glory days under Pedro’s care. Brazil’s econ- B His rule led to a slow but consistent decline in omy grew, the arts scene flourished and the emperor Brazil’s general development. steered his country to victory in a string of international C His forced exile was a direct consequence of the disputes. He also oversaw the abolition of slavery (in way he treated his daughter. 1888) while his country became increasingly attractive to disgruntled Europeans who arrived there in waves, to his ability as a ruler. looking for a better life. However, forward-thinking Pedro remained traditional in one aspect and decided his sole heir, daughter Isabel, wouldn’t inherit his throne. When the military rose up and drove him into exile, Pedro put up little resistance. Within weeks, Brazil, once the heart of the Portuguese empire, had become a republic. Wildlife in Chernobyl Question Some 116,000 people fled the radioactive fallout from 32. What is the main point? the Chernobyl power plant when its reactor exploded in 1986, and another 220,000 were later resettled. In total, A It may be a long time before humans can safely return to the area. a zone covering around 4200 square kilometres split equally between Belarus and Ukraine was vacated. In a B Wildlife in the area seems to have recovered study of wildlife in the area by researcher Jim Smith, roe since the disaster. deer, elk, wild boar and wolves were surveyed between C Wild animals are less affected by radiation than 2008 and 2010 by counting tracks left in snow. Their humans are. abundance was compared with that seen between 2005 and 2010 in four uncontaminated nature reserves of sim- for wildlife. ilar size and habitat in Belarus. The density in Cherno- byl matched or exceeded those in other reserves. A sim- ilar picture was seen when wildlife numbers in Cher- nobyl were compared with those in the Bryansky For- est reserve 250 kilometres away in Russia. “Whatever negative effects there are from radiation, they are not as large as the negative effects of having people there,” says Smith. In the Footsteps of Florence Nightingale The World Health Organisation (WHO) designated 2020 What would disappoint Nightingale in her time-travel as the “Year of the Nurse”, marking 200 years since the to the present is that the transformation of nursing has birth of Florence Nightingale, who established the prin- been uneven. In countries as varied as India, Germany ciples of modern nursing and hospital sanitation. If she and Portugal, nurses are still largely treated as doctors’ were to drop in on a hospital today, Nightingale would be minions and may not even diagnose common ailments pleased to see the progress in nursing since her day – and or prescribe medication. And although nurses make up how it is poised to change in the years to come. nearly half of the world’s health-care workforce – and Nightingale founded the first nursing school, at a hos- 90% of patients’ contacts with health workers – they pital in London in 1860, and wrote some 200 books and are often not at the table when health-policy decisions papers. She was the first woman admitted to the Royal are', '94a7e2f7beda', '["Nurses’ close connections with patients have been negatively affected by recent develop ments.","Recent changes in nursing have left traces only in some parts of the world.","The new role assigned to nurses has so far been a global success story.","Modern nursing has brought new political insights into health-care systems worldwide. Bread The earliest recognizable forms of flat bread, made from a paste of ground cereals and water, appear with the first farming communities of the Fertile Crescent, about 10,000 years ago. Although the agriculturalist diet was less nutritionally diverse than that of earlier hunter gatherers, the importance of bread in the development of human society shouldn’t be underestimated. Without the intensification of cereal production, early farming communities would have had insufficient food resources to supply those engaged in non-procurement activities, such as art and literature. Without bread, civilisation could have taken a very different turn. African Art For decades, the most prominent homes for African art were within museums in Europe. That has changed, however, especially with the recent opening of Senegal’s Museum of Black Civilizations, a repository for African art, culture and history. The $34 million project, some 50 years in the making, is designed to be a creative laboratory that will help shape the continent’s future sense of identity, according to museum director Hamady Bocoum. It also hopes to reclaim some of the continent’s lost past: the museum has room for some 18,000 art- works, but many of the galleries are not yet filled. Senegal has demanded the restitution of artworks stolen during colonial times, prompting some countries, like France, to lend pieces for the opening. John Falstaff Sir John Falstaff is one of the most famous comic charac- ters in English literature. A fat, vain, cowardly knight who is contemptuous of honourable virtues. Falstaff domi- nates the two Henry IV plays. He also acts as a “father ruf- fian” figure to the future Henry V, who is known in the plays as Prince Hal. Falstaff introduces Hal to a hedonis- tic lifestyle amongst the commoners of Eastcheap, Lon- don. Shakespeare’s depiction popularized the notion that Henry had a riotous youth before he adopted the serious mantle of kingship. It is a dramatically rich tale – but the reality is quite different."]'::jsonb, 'B', '2025vt_provpass-2-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2025vt', 2, 35),
