('What is said about clothing manufacturers in the first two paragraphs?', 'ELF', 'verbal', 'In the following text there are gaps which indicate that something has been left out. Look at the four alternatives that correspond to each gap and decide which one best fits the gap. Then mark your choice on your answer sheet. Moral Rules Alternatives Most people strictly adhere to moral rules – such as “thou shalt 31. not kill” – even when breaking them leads to a better outcome, A confusion such as sacrificing one person to save five. Is it just a bug in our B wrongdoing ethical 31 ? C condition In philosophical terms, maximizing results is called utilitar- D processing ian, whereas prioritizing rights and duties is called deontological. to, say, throw a dying man overboard to keep a life raft afloat, 32. they view someone who does such a thing as lacking empathy and integrity. A recent paper measures people’s 32 behavior toward those who make such utilitarian decisions. In several experiments, psychologists Jim Everett, Molly Crockett and David Pizarro asked American adults to respond to moral dilemmas and then interact with other supposed respondents online. When those respondents said they would 33. push a man off a footbridge to block a trolley from killing five rail workers, 33 rated them as less moral and trustworthy, A applicants and they entrusted them with less money in an investment B experimenters game. C interviewers We don’t evaluate other people on their philosophical ide- D participants ologies per se, Pizarro says. Rather, we look at how others’ moral decisions “express the kind of motives, commitments and emotions we want people to have.” Coolheaded calcula- tion has its benefits, but we want our friends to at least flinch 34. before personally 34 others. Indeed, people in the study who A encountering had argued for pushing the man were trusted more when they B judging claimed that the decision was difficult. C harming Politicians and executives should pay heed. Leading requires D disregarding making hard trade-offs: is a war or a cut in employee benefits worth the pain it inflicts? According to Pizarro, “you want your leader to genuinely have – or at least be good at displaying – the 35. right kind of 35 when they’re talking about that decision, A emotions to show that they didn’t arrive at it callously.” Calmly weighing B enthusiasm costs and benefits may do the most good for the most people, C issues but it can also be a good way to lose friends. D opinion Matthew Hutson, Scientific American Mind The century between 1700 and 1800 saw the deaths of Given its expensive and oppressive nature, one might four British monarchs, two consorts, one Prince of Wales, reasonably wonder at the longevity of state mourning, one dowager Princess of Wales and an assortment of which continued beyond the 18th century. Few would other princes and princesses. Virtually all were remem- have known the deceased well enough to feel genuine bered by a period of widespread public mourning. This sadness and there were frequent complaints about the was expressed in a variety of ways, most notably by the expense and inconvenience of mourning them. No doubt donning of primarily black clothing by huge swathes of many simply went through the motions out of deference the population, often for months at a time. to convention and a sense of loyalty to the institution The length and extent of public mourning varied and of royalty, but perhaps more important is the', 'ef9eebb990b0', '["Coloured fabrics were difficult to come by in periods of public mourning.","For many manufacturers, public mourning was economically disastrous.","Once mourning was over, stocks of expensive clothes were difficult to sell.","A period of public mourning was generally highly profitable for business."]'::jsonb, 'B', '2025vt_provpass-4-verbal-hpguiden-se.pdf', '2025vt', 4, 36),
